window.SUMMIT_READ&&window.SUMMIT_READ("p5",{"part":"<p>Part V is the content of master’s and doctoral coursework in hypersonics, propulsion and guidance. It assumes everything before it. Hypersonic flight differs from supersonic flight in kind, not just degree: the gas stops behaving as a perfect gas, boundary layers interact with shocks and with chemistry, heating dominates design, and no ground facility can reproduce flight completely. These chapters give the theory, the models, the computational and experimental tools, and the design methods.</p>","ch":{"36":{"intro":"","secs":{"36.1":"<p>With conserved variables <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{U} = \\left\\lbrack \\rho,\\ \\rho\\mathbf{u},\\ \\rho E \\right\\rbrack^{T}</span>, total energy <span class=\"mi\">E = e + \\frac{1}{2}\\left| \\mathbf{u} \\right|^{2}</span> and total enthalpy <span class=\"mi\">H = E + p/\\rho</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{U}}{\\partial t} + \\nabla \\cdot \\left( \\mathbf{F}_{inv} - \\mathbf{F}_{vis} \\right) = \\mathbf{S}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{F}_{inv} = \\left\\lbrack \\rho\\mathbf{u},\\ \\rho\\mathbf{uu} + p\\mathbf{I},\\ \\rho H\\mathbf{u} \\right\\rbrack</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{F}_{vis} = \\left\\lbrack 0,\\ \\mathbf{\\tau},\\ \\mathbf{\\tau} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} - \\mathbf{q} \\right\\rbrack</span>, closed by <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\tau}</span> (Newtonian, Chapter 18), <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{q} = - k\\nabla T</span> (plus species diffusion in mixtures) and an equation of state. Dropping the viscous terms gives the Euler equations.</p></div><p>The conservation form matters. Its weak (integral) solutions automatically satisfy the Rankine–Hugoniot jump conditions across shocks, so numerical schemes built on it capture shocks at the correct speed and strength (Chapter 45).</p>","36.2":"<p>Combining the momentum equation with the Gibbs relation gives <em>Crocco’s theorem</em>, for steady inviscid flow without body forces:</p><p><span class=\"md\">T\\nabla s = \\nabla h_{0} - \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{\\omega}.</span></p><p>Behind a <em>curved</em> shock in a uniform free stream (<span class=\"mi\">h_{0}</span> uniform), different streamlines cross the shock at different angles and gain different entropy. The resulting entropy gradients require vorticity. This is the origin of the <em>entropy layer</em> over blunt hypersonic bodies (Chapter 38). The vorticity transport equation,</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{D\\mathbf{\\omega}}{Dt} = \\left( \\mathbf{\\omega} \\cdot \\nabla \\right)\\mathbf{u} - \\mathbf{\\omega}\\left( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\right) + \\frac{\\nabla\\rho \\times \\nabla p}{\\rho^{2}} + \\nu\\nabla^{2}\\mathbf{\\omega},</span></p><p>shows the stretching, dilatation, <em>baroclinic</em> (misaligned density and pressure gradients) and diffusion mechanisms. The baroclinic term is how shocks crossing density interfaces generate mixing (Richtmyer–Meshkov instability), which is relevant to scramjet mixing and shock-tube test gas.</p>","36.3":"<p>The 1-D isentropic Euler equations are hyperbolic with characteristic speeds <span class=\"mi\">u</span> and <span class=\"mi\">u \\pm a</span>.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Riemann invariants:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">J_{\\pm} = u \\pm \\frac{2a}{\\gamma - 1}</span> are constant along the characteristics <span class=\"mi\">dx/dt = u \\pm a</span>. In a <em>simple wave</em>, one invariant is uniform everywhere. A gas at rest with sound speed <span class=\"mi\">a_{0}</span> can expand at most to the escape speed <span class=\"mi\">u_{\\max} = 2a_{0}/(\\gamma - 1)</span> (expansion into vacuum).</p></div><p>Compression characteristics converge and steepen into shocks, the mechanism of shock formation. Expansion characteristics diverge into fans. In a centered rarefaction moving into gas at rest, <span class=\"mi\">u</span> varies linearly with <span class=\"mi\">x/t</span>.</p>","36.4":"<p>A shock moving at speed <span class=\"mi\">W</span> into gas at rest is a stationary shock with <span class=\"mi\">M_{1} = M_{s} = W/a_{1}</span> in its own frame. The gas behind it moves at</p><p><span class=\"md\">u_{p} = \\frac{2a_{1}}{\\gamma + 1}\\left( M_{s} - \\frac{1}{M_{s}} \\right).</span></p><p>This is the <em>piston problem</em>: a piston pushed impulsively at <span class=\"mi\">u_{p}</span> drives a shock at <span class=\"mi\">M_{s}</span>. Shocks reflect from closed ends, bringing the gas to rest at still higher pressure and temperature.</p>","36.5":"<p>A diaphragm separates high-pressure <em>driver</em> gas (state 4) from low-pressure <em>driven</em> test gas (state 1). When it bursts, a shock runs into the test gas, an expansion fan runs into the driver, and a <em>contact surface</em> separates the two. Matching pressure and velocity across the contact gives the shock-tube equation:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{p_{4}}{p_{1}} = \\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}\\left\\lbrack 1 - \\frac{\\left( \\gamma_{4} - 1 \\right)\\left( a_{1}/a_{4} \\right)\\left( p_{2}/p_{1} - 1 \\right)}{\\sqrt{2\\gamma_{1}\\left\\lbrack 2\\gamma_{1} + \\left( \\gamma_{1} + 1 \\right)\\left( p_{2}/p_{1} - 1 \\right) \\right\\rbrack}} \\right\\rbrack^{- 2\\gamma_{4}/\\left( \\gamma_{4} - 1 \\right)}.</span></p><p>Strong shocks need a high driver sound speed <span class=\"mi\">a_{4}</span>: light, hot gases such as helium or hydrogen, heated or combustion-driven drivers, or <em>free-piston</em> compression (Stalker tubes). Test time is limited by the arrival of the contact surface and of reflected waves. In <em>tailored-interface</em> operation the reflected shock passes through the contact without generating new waves, extending test time. The <em>reflected-shock tunnel</em> expands the stagnant, high-enthalpy gas behind the reflected shock (state 5) through a nozzle. This is the workhorse facility of high-enthalpy research (Chapter 49).</p>","36.6":"<p>The method of characteristics (Chapter 24) extends to rotational and nonequilibrium flows with modified compatibility relations. <em>Shock fitting</em> tracks shocks as sharp boundaries with exact jump conditions; it is accurate but complex in topology. It contrasts with the <em>shock capturing</em> used by most CFD (Chapter 45).</p>","36.7":"<p>Writing <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u} = U_{\\infty}\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}} + \\nabla\\phi</span> with small perturbations gives the linearized potential equation <span class=\"mi\">\\left( 1 - M_{\\infty}^{2} \\right)\\phi_{xx} + \\phi_{yy} + \\phi_{zz} = 0</span>: elliptic for subsonic and hyperbolic for supersonic flow (Chapters 12 and 25). Near <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty} = 1</span> the neglected nonlinear term is essential. The <em>transonic small-disturbance equation</em></p><p><span class=\"md\">\\left\\lbrack 1 - M_{\\infty}^{2} - (\\gamma + 1)M_{\\infty}^{2}\\frac{\\phi_{x}}{U_{\\infty}} \\right\\rbrack\\phi_{xx} + \\phi_{yy} = 0</span></p><p>is of mixed type and captures embedded shocks. Linear theory also fails at high Mach number, because perturbations are no longer small compared with the free-stream sound speed.</p>","36.8":"<p>For a slender body of thickness ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\tau</span> at <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty} \\gg 1</span>, the streamwise velocity perturbation is <span class=\"mi\">O\\left( \\tau^{2}U_{\\infty} \\right)</span> while the transverse one is <span class=\"mi\">O\\left( \\tau U_{\\infty} \\right)</span>. The governing equations then depend on <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\tau</span> only through their product:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Hypersonic similarity parameter</strong> <span class=\"mi\">K = M_{\\infty}\\tau</span>. Affine slender bodies with the same <span class=\"mi\">K</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span> have the same <span class=\"mi\">p/p_{\\infty}</span> distribution, and <span class=\"mi\">C_{p}/\\tau^{2}</span> is a function of <span class=\"mi\">K</span> alone. <strong>Equivalence principle</strong> (Hayes): steady hypersonic flow past a slender body is equivalent to unsteady flow in each cross-flow plane, driven by the body acting as an expanding piston. This leads to blast-wave theory (Chapter 38).</p></div><p>Example: a wedge with <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = 0.1</span> at <span class=\"mi\">M = 10</span> and one with <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = 0.05</span> at <span class=\"mi\">M = 20</span> (both <span class=\"mi\">K = 1</span>) have exact pressure ratios of 3.47 and 3.47.</p>","36.9":"<p>At high temperature the caloric equation <span class=\"mi\">e = c_{v}T</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = \\text{const}</span> fail. The thermodynamic state needs <span class=\"mi\">h\\left( T,p,\\text{composition} \\right)</span>, and acoustic waves propagate at the <em>frozen</em> sound speed or the <em>equilibrium</em> sound speed (lower), depending on whether chemistry keeps pace with the wave. Chapters 39 and 40 build this physics.</p>"}},"37":{"intro":"","secs":{"37.1":"<p>In a layer of thickness <span class=\"mi\">\\delta \\sim L\\, Re^{- 1/2}</span>, an order-of-magnitude analysis of Navier–Stokes leaves (2-D, steady, incompressible)</p><p><span class=\"md\">uu_{x} + vu_{y} = - \\frac{1}{\\rho}p_{x} + \\nu u_{yy},\\quad\\quad p_{y} = 0,\\quad\\quad u_{x} + v_{y} = 0.</span></p><p>The pressure is impressed by the outer inviscid flow, and the equations are <em>parabolic</em>: they march downstream. Integral thicknesses: displacement <span class=\"mi\">\\delta^{*} = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\left( 1 - \\frac{\\rho u}{\\rho_{e}u_{e}} \\right)dy</span>; momentum <span class=\"mi\">\\theta = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{\\rho u}{\\rho_{e}u_{e}}\\left( 1 - \\frac{u}{u_{e}} \\right)dy</span>; shape factor <span class=\"mi\">H = \\delta^{*}/\\theta</span> (2.59 for Blasius, about 1.3–1.4 turbulent, rising toward separation). The <em>von Kármán momentum integral</em> holds for any boundary layer:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{d\\theta}{dx} + (2 + H)\\frac{\\theta}{u_{e}}\\frac{du_{e}}{dx} = \\frac{c_{f}}{2}.</span></p>","37.2":"<p>With <span class=\"mi\">\\eta = y\\sqrt{U/(\\nu x)}</span> and stream function <span class=\"mi\">\\psi = \\sqrt{\\nu Ux}f(\\eta)</span>, the flat-plate (Blasius) problem becomes <span class=\"mi\">f''' + \\frac{1}{2}ff'' = 0</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">f(0) = f'(0) = 0</span> and <span class=\"mi\">f'(\\infty) = 1</span>, giving the results of Chapter 18. Falkner–Skan solutions treat <span class=\"mi\">u_{e} \\propto x^{m}</span>; the case <span class=\"mi\">m = 1</span> is the stagnation-point flow used in heating theory (Chapter 41).</p>","37.3":"<p>Viscous dissipation heats the layer. An insulated wall reaches the <em>adiabatic wall (recovery) temperature</em>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">T_{aw} = T_{e}\\left( 1 + r\\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M_{e}^{2} \\right)</span>, with recovery factor <span class=\"mi\">r \\approx Pr^{1/2}</span> (laminar, 0.84 for air) or <span class=\"mi\">Pr^{1/3}</span> (turbulent, 0.89). Heat flux to a wall at <span class=\"mi\">T_{w}</span>: <span class=\"mi\">q_{w} = St\\,\\rho_{e}u_{e}c_{p}\\left( T_{aw} - T_{w} \\right)</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">St</span> from the Reynolds analogy (Chapter 19). <strong>Reference temperature method</strong> (Eckert, as given by Anderson for γ = 1.4): evaluate incompressible <span class=\"mi\">c_{f}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">St</span> formulas with properties at <span class=\"mi\">T^{*} = T_{e}\\left\\lbrack 1 + 0.032M_{e}^{2} + 0.58\\left( T_{w}/T_{e} - 1 \\right) \\right\\rbrack</span>.</p></div><p>For <span class=\"mi\">\\Pr = 1</span> and zero pressure gradient, temperature and velocity are linked by the <em>Crocco–Busemann relation</em>. In the general (Walz) form:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{T}{T_{e}} = \\frac{T_{w}}{T_{e}} + \\frac{T_{aw} - T_{w}}{T_{e}}\\frac{u}{u_{e}} - r\\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M_{e}^{2}\\left( \\frac{u}{u_{e}} \\right)^{2}.</span></p><p>Hypersonic vehicles usually have <em>cold walls</em> (<span class=\"mi\">T_{w} \\ll T_{aw}</span>): the dense near-wall gas thins the boundary layer and raises the heat flux. Compressibility transformations (Howarth–Dorodnitsyn for laminar flow; Van Driest II for turbulent skin friction) map compressible layers to incompressible ones.</p>","37.4":"<p>Compressible turbulence is averaged with density weighting (Favre averaging, <span class=\"mi\">\\widetilde{u} = \\overline{\\rho u}/\\bar{\\rho}</span>), producing Reynolds stresses <span class=\"mi\">- \\bar{\\rho}\\widetilde{u_{i}''u_{j}''}</span> and turbulent heat fluxes. <em>Morkovin’s hypothesis</em>: for moderate Mach numbers (<span class=\"mi\">M_{e} \\lesssim 5</span>), compressibility affects wall turbulence mainly through mean property variations. The <em>law of the wall</em>, with <span class=\"mi\">u^{+} = u/u_{\\tau}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">y^{+} = yu_{\\tau}/\\nu_{w}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">u_{\\tau} = \\sqrt{\\tau_{w}/\\rho_{w}}</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">u^{+} = y^{+}\\ \\left( y^{+} &lt; 5 \\right),\\quad\\quad u^{+} = \\frac{1}{\\kappa}\\ln y^{+} + B\\ \\left( y^{+} \\gtrsim 30 \\right),\\quad\\quad\\kappa = 0.41,\\ B \\approx 5.0.</span></p><p>For compressible flow it holds in Van Driest-transformed velocity, <span class=\"mi\">u_{VD}^{+} = \\int_{0}^{u^{+}}\\sqrt{\\rho/\\rho_{w}}\\, du^{+}</span>.</p>","37.5":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Approach</th> <th>Examples</th> <th>Strengths</th> <th>Weaknesses in hypersonics</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Algebraic eddy viscosity</td> <td>Baldwin–Lomax, Cebeci–Smith</td> <td>Cheap</td> <td>No history effects; poor in separation</td> </tr> <tr> <td>One-equation</td> <td>Spalart–Allmaras</td> <td>Robust; aerospace standard for attached flow</td> <td>Separation and heat flux in interactions</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Two-equation</td> <td>k–ε, Wilcox k–ω, Menter SST</td> <td>Broad applicability</td> <td>Shock-unsteadiness, compressibility and heat-transfer errors</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Reynolds-stress</td> <td>SSG/LRR</td> <td>Anisotropy, curvature</td> <td>Cost, robustness</td> </tr> <tr> <td>LES / hybrid RANS–LES</td> <td>Dynamic Smagorinsky; DES/DDES/IDDES</td> <td>Resolve large eddies and unsteadiness</td> <td>Cost; near-wall modeling; inflow generation</td> </tr> <tr> <td>DNS</td> <td>—</td> <td>No model</td> <td>Cost grows as roughly <span class=\"mi\">Re^{37/14}</span></td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Heat transfer additionally requires a turbulent Prandtl number (<span class=\"mi\">Pr_{t} \\approx 0.85</span>–0.9), itself a source of uncertainty. Grid-point estimates for a boundary layer (Choi and Moin): wall-resolved LES <span class=\"mi\">\\sim Re_{L}^{13/7}</span>, wall-modeled LES <span class=\"mi\">\\sim Re_{L}</span>, DNS <span class=\"mi\">\\sim Re_{L}^{37/14}</span>. RANS models typically mispredict peak heating and separation length in shock–boundary-layer interactions by tens of percent, an open problem (Chapter 53).</p>","37.6":"<p>Transition decides whether a vehicle sees laminar or turbulent heating, a factor of three to eight (Chapter 41). <strong>Linear stability theory</strong> superposes normal modes <span class=\"mi\">\\widehat{q}(y)\\exp\\left\\lbrack i(\\alpha x + \\beta z - \\omega t) \\right\\rbrack</span> on the laminar profile. Incompressible flow obeys the Orr–Sommerfeld equation. <em>Rayleigh’s criterion</em>: inviscid instability requires an inflection point in <span class=\"mi\">u(y)</span>; for compressible flow, a generalized inflection point where <span class=\"mi\">(\\rho u')' = 0</span> (Lees–Lin).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Instability mechanisms at high speed.</strong> <em>First mode</em>: vortical, most unstable as oblique waves at supersonic speeds; stabilized by wall cooling. <em>Mack second mode</em>: acoustic waves trapped between the wall and the relative sonic line; dominant for <span class=\"mi\">M_{e} \\gtrsim 4</span>; wavelength about twice the boundary-layer thickness, so <span class=\"mi\">f \\approx u_{e}/(2\\delta)</span> (often 100 kHz–1 MHz); <strong>destabilized</strong> by wall cooling. <em>Crossflow</em>: swept or three-dimensional flows such as cones at incidence. <em>Görtler</em>: concave curvature. <em>Attachment-line contamination</em>: swept leading edges. <em>Entropy-layer instabilities</em>: blunt bodies.</p></div><p><em>Receptivity</em> is how external disturbances (free-stream acoustic noise, vorticity, roughness, particles) seed the instabilities. <em>Transient (non-modal) growth</em> can bypass modal paths. The <strong>e^N method</strong> integrates linear growth rates to an amplification <span class=\"mi\">N = ln\\left( A/A_{0} \\right)</span> and correlates transition with a critical <span class=\"mi\">N</span>. Values of about 9–10 suit quiet, flight-like environments; conventional noisy wind tunnels give much lower values. Quiet tunnels (for example the Boeing/AFOSR Mach-6 Quiet Tunnel at Purdue) keep their walls laminar to reproduce flight-like noise levels. Parabolized stability equations add non-parallel and nonlinear effects. Isolated roughness trips transition when the roughness Reynolds number <span class=\"mi\">Re_{kk} = \\rho_{k}u_{k}k/\\mu_{k}</span> exceeds a critical value of order hundreds. RANS-compatible transition models (amplification-factor transport, γ–Re_θ) are calibrated, not predictive, outside their data.</p>","37.7":"<p>The pressure rise needed to separate a boundary layer depends mainly on the conditions at the start of the interaction, not on what caused it (<em>free interaction</em>, Chapman). A compression ramp or impinging shock above a critical strength separates the layer. A separation bubble forms with a λ-shock structure, and heating peaks at <em>reattachment</em>, roughly scaling as <span class=\"mi\">q_{pk}/q_{0} \\approx \\left( p_{pk}/p_{0} \\right)^{0.8\\text{–}0.85}</span> for turbulent flow. Turbulent interactions also show low-frequency unsteadiness that loads structures. SWBLI governs control-surface heating, inlet behavior (Chapter 44) and much of the remaining uncertainty in vehicle design.</p>","37.8":"<p>At high <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}</span> and low <span class=\"mi\">Re</span>, the thick, hot boundary layer displaces the outer flow enough to raise the surface pressure. The governing parameter is</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\bar{\\chi} = M_{\\infty}^{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{C}{Re_{x}}},\\quad\\quad C = \\frac{\\rho_{w}\\mu_{w}}{\\rho_{\\infty}\\mu_{\\infty}}\\ \\text{(Chapman–Rubesin)}.</span></p><p>For an insulated flat plate (γ = 1.4): weak interaction (<span class=\"mi\">\\bar{\\chi} \\lesssim 3</span>), <span class=\"mi\">p/p_{\\infty} \\approx 1 + 0.31\\bar{\\chi} + 0.05{\\bar{\\chi}}^{2}</span>; strong interaction (<span class=\"mi\">\\bar{\\chi} \\gtrsim 3</span>), <span class=\"mi\">p/p_{\\infty} \\approx 0.514\\bar{\\chi} + 0.759</span>. The rarefaction parameter <span class=\"mi\">\\bar{V} = M_{\\infty}\\sqrt{C/Re_{x}}</span> governs skin friction and heating corrections. Very near the leading edge, the shock and boundary layer merge, and the flow becomes rarefied (Chapter 40).</p>"}},"38":{"intro":"","secs":{"38.1":"<p>Hypersonic flow is defined not by a Mach number but by five phenomena that become important together, roughly above Mach 5:</p><ol type=\"1\"> <li><strong>Thin shock layers:</strong> the shock lies close to the body, and at low Reynolds number it merges with the boundary layer.</li> <li><strong>Entropy layers:</strong> curved shocks around blunt noses create layers of strong entropy gradient and vorticity that wash over the downstream body (Chapter 36).</li> <li><strong>Viscous interaction:</strong> thick, hot boundary layers displace the outer flow (Chapter 37).</li> <li><strong>High-temperature effects:</strong> vibrational excitation, dissociation, ionization, chemical nonequilibrium and radiation (Chapters 39–41).</li> <li><strong>Low-density effects:</strong> slip and rarefaction at high altitude (Chapter 40).</li> </ol>","38.2":"<p>As <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty} \\rightarrow \\infty</span> the oblique-shock relations simplify:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{\\infty}} \\rightarrow \\frac{2\\gamma}{\\gamma + 1}M_{\\infty}^{2}\\sin^{2}\\beta</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\frac{\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{\\infty}} \\rightarrow \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{\\gamma - 1}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad C_{p} \\rightarrow \\frac{4}{\\gamma + 1}\\sin^{2}\\beta</span>. For a thin wedge with <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}\\theta \\gg 1</span>: <span class=\"mi\">\\beta \\approx \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2}\\theta</span>, so the shock lies only 20% beyond the surface for γ = 1.4. In general, <span class=\"mi\">C_{p}/\\tau^{2} = \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2} + \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2} \\right)^{2} + \\frac{4}{K^{2}}}</span> with <span class=\"mi\">K = M_{\\infty}\\tau</span> (hypersonic small-disturbance wedge result).</p></div>","38.3":"<p>Newton modeled the flow as particles that lose their normal momentum on striking the surface, giving <span class=\"mi\">C_{p} = 2\\sin^{2}\\theta</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\theta</span> is the angle between the free stream and the local surface. The theory becomes exact as <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty} \\rightarrow \\infty</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma \\rightarrow 1</span>, when the shock layer becomes infinitely thin. <strong>Modified Newtonian</strong> (Lees) replaces 2 with the stagnation value behind a normal shock:</p><p><span class=\"md\">C_{p} = C_{p,\\max}\\sin^{2}\\theta,\\quad\\quad C_{p,\\max} = \\frac{p_{02} - p_{\\infty}}{q_{\\infty}} \\rightarrow 1.839\\ \\left( \\gamma = 1.4,\\ M_{\\infty} \\rightarrow \\infty \\right).</span></p><p>Surfaces hidden from the flow (“shadow”) get <span class=\"mi\">C_{p} \\approx 0</span>. The <em>Newton–Busemann</em> correction subtracts centrifugal effects on curved surfaces. Classic results, with <span class=\"mi\">C_{p,\\max} = 2</span>:</p><ul> <li><strong>Flat plate at angle of attack</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>: <span class=\"mi\">C_{N} = 2\\sin^{2}\\alpha</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{L} = 2\\sin^{2}\\alpha\\cos\\alpha</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} = 2\\sin^{3}\\alpha</span>, <span class=\"mi\">L/D = cot\\alpha</span>. Lift is maximal at <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha = {54.7}^{\\circ}</span>.</li> <li><strong>Sphere:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} = 1</span> on the frontal area (about 0.92 with modified Newtonian).</li> <li><strong>Cone</strong> of half-angle <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{c}</span> at zero incidence: <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} = 2\\sin^{2}\\theta_{c}</span>, with the normal-force center of pressure at two-thirds of the length from the apex.</li> </ul><p>Newtonian theory is best for blunt, high-inclination surfaces and poor for slender shapes at moderate <span class=\"mi\">K</span>, where tangent-wedge and tangent-cone methods are better.</p>","38.4":"<p>Engineering codes assign each panel a pressure from its local inclination: the tangent-wedge method (2-D, exact oblique-shock pressure at the local angle), the tangent-cone method (axisymmetric, conical-flow pressure, Appendix F), shock-expansion theory, or modified Newtonian on blunt regions. These methods give forces and moments in milliseconds and remain essential for conceptual design, trajectory optimization and early database generation, alongside CFD.</p>","38.5":"<p>As <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty} \\rightarrow \\infty</span> (for a calorically perfect gas), <span class=\"mi\">p/p_{\\infty}</span> grows as <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}^{2}</span>, but the <em>coefficients</em> <span class=\"mi\">C_{p}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{L}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{D}</span> and the shock shape approach limits independent of <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}</span> (Oswatitsch). This is visible above about Mach 10 for blunt bodies. It justifies extrapolating coefficients measured at Mach 6–10, but only for the perfect-gas part of the problem: real-gas effects break the independence (Chapter 43).</p>","38.6":"<p>A strong explosion of energy <span class=\"mi\">E</span> in a gas of density <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{\\infty}</span> drives a self-similar shock, <span class=\"mi\">R(t) \\propto \\left( E/\\rho_{\\infty} \\right)^{1/(j + 3)}t^{2/(j + 3)}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">j = 0</span> (planar), 1 (cylindrical) or 2 (spherical). By the equivalence principle (Chapter 36), the nose drag of a blunt-nosed slender body acts as the “explosion” in each cross-flow plane. For γ = 1.4:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Body</th> <th>Induced surface pressure</th> <th>Shock shape</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Blunt flat plate (planar blast)</td> <td><span class=\"md\">p/p_{\\infty} = 0.121M_{\\infty}^{2}C_{D}^{2/3}(x/d)^{- 2/3}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">y/d = 0.774C_{D}^{1/3}(x/d)^{2/3}</span></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Blunt cylinder (cylindrical blast)</td> <td><span class=\"md\">p/p_{\\infty} = 0.067M_{\\infty}^{2}C_{D}^{1/2}(x/d)^{- 1}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">r/d = 0.795C_{D}^{1/4}(x/d)^{1/2}</span></td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>These hold where the induced pressure greatly exceeds <span class=\"mi\">p_{\\infty}</span>, a few diameters downstream of the nose. They explain why nose bluntness raises pressure, and therefore drag and heating, far downstream.</p>","38.7":"<p>Behind the near-normal part of a bow shock the flow is subsonic, so the blunt-body problem is elliptic there and was historically hard. Today it is solved by time-marching CFD. Billig’s correlations give the standoff distance <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta</span> and the shock curvature <span class=\"mi\">R_{c}</span> at the axis:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\text{sphere: }\\frac{\\Delta}{R} = 0.143e^{3.24/M_{\\infty}^{2}},\\ \\frac{R_{c}}{R} = 1.143e^{0.54/\\left( M_{\\infty} - 1 \\right)^{1.2}};\\quad\\quad\\text{cylinder: }\\frac{\\Delta}{R} = 0.386e^{4.67/M_{\\infty}^{2}},\\ \\frac{R_{c}}{R} = 1.386e^{1.8/\\left( M_{\\infty} - 1 \\right)^{0.75}},</span></p><p>with a hyperbolic shock shape <span class=\"mi\">x = R + \\Delta - R_{c}\\cot^{2}\\beta\\left\\lbrack \\sqrt{1 + y^{2}\\tan^{2}\\beta/R_{c}^{2}} - 1 \\right\\rbrack</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\beta</span> is the asymptotic shock angle (the free-stream Mach angle). In dissociating gas the density ratio across the shock rises, and for spheres <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta/R \\approx 0.78\\,\\rho_{\\infty}/\\rho_{2}</span>: the shock layer thins. The <em>entropy layer</em> generated by the nose is swallowed by the growing boundary layer over a distance that depends on bluntness and Reynolds number. Nose bluntness delays transition, up to a point beyond which transition “reverses” and moves forward again, an active research topic.</p>","38.8":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Configuration</th> <th>Hypersonic L/D</th> <th>Character</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Ballistic sphere or sphere-cone</td> <td>≈ 0</td> <td>Maximum drag, no control</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Capsule with CG offset</td> <td>0.25–0.4</td> <td>Trimmed at incidence; bank-angle steering</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Slender cone or biconic</td> <td>1–2.5</td> <td>Low drag; higher heating at nose</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Lifting body / winged orbiter</td> <td>1–2</td> <td>Cross-range; complex heating</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Waverider</td> <td>3–6+</td> <td>Shock attached along leading edges; high L/D; low volume</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Küchemann’s empirical “L/D barrier” for conventional shapes is <span class=\"mi\">(L/D)_{\\max} \\approx 4\\left( M_{\\infty} + 3 \\right)/M_{\\infty}</span>. Skin friction becomes a large share of drag, so viscous effects limit what inviscid design can promise. Volume efficiency, measured by Küchemann’s <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = V/S_{plan}^{3/2}</span>, competes with L/D.</p>","38.9":"<p>Capsules trim at an angle of attack set by a lateral CG offset, flying at modest L/D with static stability provided by the CG lying ahead of the aerodynamic center. Slender bodies gain stability from flares or fins. Real-gas effects shift pressure distributions and hence trim. The Shuttle’s first flight needed about twice the predicted body-flap deflection for this reason (Chapter 43).</p>"}},"39":{"intro":"","secs":{"39.1":"<p>Behind a normal shock at Mach 25 (orbital entry, <span class=\"mi\">T_{\\infty} \\approx 220</span> K), perfect-gas theory predicts about 27 000 K. The real equilibrium temperature is only about 6000–7000 K, because the energy goes into molecular vibration, dissociation and ionization rather than into translational motion. These processes absorb energy, change the molar mass and <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span>, increase the density behind shocks, alter heating and radiation, and, when they lag the flow, create nonequilibrium (Chapter 40).</p>","39.2":"<p>The energy of a molecule is the sum of translational, rotational, vibrational and electronic parts. Classical equipartition gives <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{1}{2}kT</span> per quadratic energy term: translation <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{3}{2}kT</span>, rotation of a linear molecule <span class=\"mi\">kT</span> (fully excited above a few kelvin; characteristic rotational temperatures N₂ 2.88 K, O₂ 2.08 K). Vibration is quantized: <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{v} = \\left( v + \\frac{1}{2} \\right)h\\nu</span>, with characteristic temperature <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{v} = h\\nu/k</span> (N₂ 3393 K, O₂ 2274 K, NO 2740 K).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Harmonic-oscillator vibrational energy</strong> per unit mass: <span class=\"mi\">e_{vib} = \\frac{R\\theta_{v}}{e^{\\theta_{v}/T} - 1}</span>, approaching <span class=\"mi\">RT</span> (two quadratic terms) for <span class=\"mi\">T \\gg \\theta_{v}</span>. Its heat capacity is <span class=\"mi\">c_{v,vib}/R = \\frac{\\left( \\theta_{v}/T \\right)^{2}e^{\\theta_{v}/T}}{\\left( e^{\\theta_{v}/T} - 1 \\right)^{2}}</span>. A diatomic gas goes from <span class=\"mi\">c_{v} = \\frac{5}{2}R</span> (<span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = 1.4</span>) toward <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{7}{2}R</span> (<span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = 9/7 \\approx 1.29</span>) as vibration is excited.</p></div><p>Electronic excitation matters for atoms with low-lying levels (atomic oxygen) and for radiation.</p>","39.3":"<p>In equilibrium, the populations of energy states follow the <strong>Boltzmann distribution</strong> <span class=\"mi\">N_{j}/N = g_{j}e^{- \\varepsilon_{j}/kT}/Q</span>, with degeneracy <span class=\"mi\">g_{j}</span> and partition function <span class=\"mi\">Q = \\sum_{j}^{}g_{j}e^{- \\varepsilon_{j}/kT}</span>. For independent modes, <span class=\"mi\">Q = Q_{tr}Q_{rot}Q_{vib}Q_{el}</span>, with</p><p><span class=\"md\">Q_{tr} = \\left( \\frac{2\\pi mkT}{h^{2}} \\right)^{3/2}V,\\quad\\quad Q_{rot} = \\frac{T}{\\sigma\\theta_{r}},\\quad\\quad Q_{vib} = \\frac{1}{1 - e^{- \\theta_{v}/T}},\\quad\\quad Q_{el} = \\sum_{i}^{}g_{i}e^{- \\theta_{el,i}/T}.</span></p><p>Here <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma</span> is the symmetry number (2 for homonuclear, 1 for heteronuclear molecules), and energies are measured from the ground vibrational level. All thermodynamic properties follow from <span class=\"mi\">Q</span>: for example <span class=\"mi\">e = RT^{2}(\\partial lnQ/\\partial T)_{V}</span> per unit mass.</p>","39.4":"<p>For dissociation <span class=\"mi\">A_{2} \\rightleftharpoons 2A</span>, statistical mechanics gives</p><p><span class=\"md\">K_{c} = \\frac{n_{A}^{2}}{n_{A_{2}}} = \\frac{\\left( Q_{A}/V \\right)^{2}}{Q_{A_{2}}/V}e^{- D_{0}/kT},\\quad\\quad K_{p} = K_{c}kT,</span></p><p>where <span class=\"mi\">D_{0}</span> is the dissociation energy from the ground vibrational state. <em>Worked example:</em> for O₂ ⇌ 2O at 3000 K, with <span class=\"mi\">D_{0} = 5.116</span> eV, <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{r} = 2.08</span> K, <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{v} = 2274</span> K and the electronic levels of O and O₂, this gives <span class=\"mi\">K_{p} \\approx 0.013</span> atm, in agreement with the JANAF tables. The <em>Saha equation</em> plays the same role for ionization:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{n_{e}n_{i}}{n_{n}} = \\left( \\frac{2\\pi m_{e}kT}{h^{2}} \\right)^{3/2}\\frac{2Q_{i}}{Q_{n}}e^{- I/kT}.</span></p>","39.5":"<p>At 1 atm, O₂ dissociation begins near 2500 K and is nearly complete by about 4000 K. N₂ dissociation begins near 4000 K and is nearly complete by about 9000 K. NO forms at intermediate temperatures (a few percent). Ionization becomes significant above about 9000 K. Lower pressure shifts all thresholds downward (Le Chatelier). Standard models are 5-species (N₂, O₂, NO, N, O), 7-species (adding NO⁺ and e⁻) and 11-species (adding N₂⁺, O₂⁺, N⁺ and O⁺). Equilibrium properties are presented as <span class=\"mi\">h(p,T)</span>, Mollier charts or curve fits. The effective ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma_{eff} = h/e</span> falls to about 1.1–1.2 in dissociating air.</p>","39.6":"<p>Solve iteratively. Guess <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon = \\rho_{1}/\\rho_{2}</span> (start near 0.1). From the conservation laws,</p><p><span class=\"md\">p_{2} = p_{1} + \\rho_{1}u_{1}^{2}(1 - \\varepsilon),\\quad\\quad h_{2} = h_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}u_{1}^{2}\\left( 1 - \\varepsilon^{2} \\right).</span></p><p>Then find <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{2}\\left( p_{2},h_{2} \\right)</span> from equilibrium properties, update <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span>, and repeat to convergence. Compared with perfect-gas results, <span class=\"mi\">T_{2}</span> is far lower, <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{2}/\\rho_{1}</span> reaches 10–20, and <span class=\"mi\">p_{2}</span> changes only slightly, because <span class=\"mi\">p_{2} \\approx \\rho_{1}u_{1}^{2}(1 - \\varepsilon)</span> is insensitive to <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span> when <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span> is small. That is why Newtonian pressure estimates survive real-gas effects while temperature and heating estimates do not.</p>","39.7":"<p>Kinetic theory (Chapman–Enskog) gives the viscosity of a pure gas as <span class=\"mi\">\\mu = \\frac{5}{16}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi mkT}}{\\pi\\sigma^{2}\\Omega^{(2,2)*}}</span>, with collision integrals <span class=\"mi\">\\Omega^{*}</span> computed from intermolecular potentials. Mixtures use Wilke or Gupta–Yos mixing rules. Thermal conductivity includes internal-mode contributions (Eucken correction) and, in reacting gas, a <em>reactive</em> conductivity: atoms diffuse toward cooler regions and release recombination energy there. Species diffusion follows the Stefan–Maxwell relations, often simplified to Fick’s law with an effective Lewis number of about 1.4 for air atoms. Diffusion of atoms to a catalytic wall is a major heat-transfer path (Chapter 41).</p>","39.8":"<p>To compute rocket performance: (1) find the chamber equilibrium at <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> and the reactant enthalpy by Gibbs minimization (Chapter 17); (2) expand isentropically to <span class=\"mi\">p_{e}</span>, either recomputing the equilibrium at each pressure (shifting) or holding the composition fixed (frozen); (3) obtain <span class=\"mi\">u_{e} = \\sqrt{2\\left( h_{c} - h_{e} \\right)}</span>. Shifting expansion recovers recombination energy, so its Isp is higher. Real nozzles freeze partway, roughly where the expansion rate outruns the recombination rate (Bray’s criterion), which places the result between the two limits.</p>","39.9":"<p>High-temperature gases emit through bound–bound transitions (atomic N and O lines; molecular bands such as N₂⁺ first negative, NO β and γ, and CN violet in CO₂ atmospheres), bound–free and free–free transitions. A gas may be optically thin (emission escapes) or thick (self-absorbing). Shock-layer radiation becomes a major heating component for Earth entry above about 10 km/s (Chapter 41).</p>"}},"40":{"intro":"","secs":{"40.1":"<div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">Da = \\frac{\\tau_{flow}}{\\tau_{chem}}</span> (with <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{flow} = L/V</span>). <span class=\"mi\">Da \\gg 1</span>: chemistry keeps pace (<em>local equilibrium</em>). <span class=\"mi\">Da \\ll 1</span>: composition is <em>frozen</em>. <span class=\"mi\">Da \\sim 1</span>: <em>nonequilibrium</em>, needing finite-rate models. The same test applies to vibrational relaxation with <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{vib}</span>.</p></div><p><strong>Binary scaling.</strong> Dissociation proceeds by two-body collisions, so the extent of reaction over a body depends on <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{\\infty}L</span>. A subscale model at the same velocity and the same <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{\\infty}L</span> reproduces nonequilibrium dissociation. Recombination is a three-body process (<span class=\"mi\">\\propto \\rho^{2}</span>) and breaks binary scaling. No single facility condition reproduces both (Chapter 49).</p>","40.2":"<p>Molecular speeds in equilibrium follow the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution, <span class=\"mi\">f\\left( \\mathbf{c} \\right) = n\\left( \\frac{m}{2\\pi kT} \\right)^{3/2}\\exp\\left( - \\frac{mc^{2}}{2kT} \\right)</span>, with mean speed <span class=\"mi\">\\bar{c} = \\sqrt{8kT/(\\pi m)}</span>. For hard spheres of diameter <span class=\"mi\">d</span>, the mean free path is</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\lambda = \\frac{kT}{\\sqrt{2}\\pi d^{2}p} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}n\\pi d^{2}},</span></p><p>about 68 nm in sea-level air and about 0.14 m at 100 km. The collision frequency is <span class=\"mi\">\\bar{c}/\\lambda</span>. Elementary kinetic theory gives <span class=\"mi\">\\mu \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\rho\\bar{c}\\lambda</span>; Chapman–Enskog theory (Chapter 39) gives accurate values.</p>","40.3":"<p>The <em>Landau–Teller</em> equation relaxes vibrational energy toward its equilibrium value at the translational temperature: <span class=\"mi\">de_{v}/dt = \\left\\lbrack e_{v}^{*}(T) - e_{v} \\right\\rbrack/\\tau_{v}</span>. The relaxation time follows the <strong>Millikan–White</strong> correlation,</p><p><span class=\"md\">p\\tau_{MW} = exp\\left\\lbrack A\\left( T^{- 1/3} - 0.015\\,{\\widetilde{\\mu}}^{1/4} \\right) - 18.42 \\right\\rbrack\\ \\ (atm \\cdot s),\\quad\\quad A = 1.16 \\times 10^{- 3}{\\widetilde{\\mu}}^{1/2}\\theta_{v}^{4/3},</span></p><p>where <span class=\"mi\">\\widetilde{\\mu}</span> is the collision pair’s reduced mass in atomic mass units. At very high temperature, Millikan–White gives unphysically short times. Park adds a collision-limited term <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{P} = 1/\\left( n\\sigma_{v}\\bar{c} \\right)</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{v} = 3 \\times 10^{- 21}(50\\, 000/T)^{2}</span> m², and uses <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{v} = \\tau_{MW} + \\tau_{P}</span>.</p>","40.4":"<p>Each species obeys a continuity equation with diffusion and a chemical source, <span class=\"mi\">\\partial\\left( \\rho Y_{i} \\right)/\\partial t + \\nabla \\cdot \\left( \\rho Y_{i}\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{J}_{i} \\right) = {\\dot{\\omega}}_{i}</span>. The source is built from law-of-mass-action rates (Chapter 17). Air mechanisms (for example Park’s) include dissociation of N₂, O₂ and NO by every collision partner; the Zeldovich exchange reactions N₂ + O ⇌ NO + N and NO + O ⇌ O₂ + N; and, at higher speeds, associative ionization N + O ⇌ NO⁺ + e⁻, the first source of electrons. High-temperature rate coefficients are uncertain by factors of 2 to 10, a leading source of uncertainty in heating and radiation predictions.</p>","40.5":"<p>When vibration lags translation, a single temperature cannot describe the gas. <strong>Park’s two-temperature model</strong> carries <span class=\"mi\">T</span> (translation and rotation) and <span class=\"mi\">T_{v}</span> (vibration, electrons and electronic excitation). Dissociation rates are evaluated at a controlling temperature <span class=\"mi\">T_{a} = T^{q}T_{v}^{1 - q}</span> (commonly <span class=\"mi\">q = 0.5</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{TT_{v}}</span>), because molecules dissociate preferentially from high vibrational levels. Coupled vibration–dissociation models (such as Treanor–Marrone) account for the vibrational energy removed by dissociation. Ionized flows add an electron temperature <span class=\"mi\">T_{e}</span>. The source terms couple through Landau–Teller exchange, chemistry–vibration terms and electron–vibration terms.</p>","40.6":"<p>Behind a strong shock, translation equilibrates within a few collisions, giving a <em>frozen</em> jump with γ ≈ 1.4 and a very high translational temperature. Vibration and dissociation then absorb energy over the <em>relaxation zone</em>: <span class=\"mi\">T</span> falls, <span class=\"mi\">T_{v}</span> rises, and both approach equilibrium. Radiation from this zone can <em>overshoot</em> the equilibrium value. In rapidly expanding nozzles, recombination and vibrational de-excitation freeze. The free streams of arc jets and reflected-shock tunnels are therefore partly dissociated and vibrationally excited, which complicates simulation of flight (Chapter 49). Nonequilibrium also changes heating (through wall catalysis, Chapter 41), aerodynamic moments (Chapter 43) and scramjet ignition (Chapter 44).</p>","40.7":"<p>Quantum chemistry provides <em>potential energy surfaces</em> for molecular collisions. Quasi-classical trajectory (QCT) calculations on those surfaces give state-specific rates, and direct molecular simulation tracks ensembles of collisions directly. Master equations with coarse-grained energy bins evolve non-Boltzmann distributions. These methods have shown that dissociation proceeds through a quasi-steady, depleted high-vibrational population, and they are steadily replacing empirical rates. This is an active research frontier (Chapter 53).</p>","40.8":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Regime</th> <th>Knudsen number <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{Kn}\\mathbf{=}\\mathbf{\\lambda}\\mathbf{/}\\mathbf{L}</span></th> <th>Model</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Continuum</td> <td><span class=\"md\">&lt; 0.001</span></td> <td>Navier–Stokes with no-slip walls</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Slip</td> <td>0.001–0.1</td> <td>Navier–Stokes with velocity-slip and temperature-jump walls</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Transitional</td> <td>0.1–10</td> <td>Boltzmann equation; DSMC</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Free molecular</td> <td><span class=\"md\">&gt; 10</span></td> <td>Collisionless kinetic theory</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>A <em>local</em> Knudsen number based on gradient length scales, <span class=\"mi\">Kn_{GLL} = (\\lambda/Q)|\\nabla Q|</span>, detects continuum breakdown inside shocks, boundary layers and wakes even when the global <span class=\"mi\">Kn</span> is small. The <strong>Boltzmann equation</strong>, <span class=\"mi\">\\partial f/\\partial t + \\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\nabla f + \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\nabla_{c}f = (\\partial f/\\partial t)_{coll}</span>, governs the velocity distribution <span class=\"mi\">f</span>. The BGK model approximates collisions as relaxation toward a Maxwellian, and the Chapman–Enskog expansion recovers Navier–Stokes at first order in <span class=\"mi\">Kn</span>. Maxwell’s slip condition is <span class=\"mi\">u_{s} = \\frac{2 - \\sigma}{\\sigma}\\lambda\\left( \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} \\right)_{w}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma</span> is the tangential momentum accommodation coefficient, with an analogous temperature jump.</p>","40.9":"<p>DSMC (Bird) represents the gas by simulated particles, each standing for many molecules. Each time step it (1) moves particles ballistically; (2) applies surface interactions (diffuse or specular reflection with accommodation); (3) sorts particles into cells; (4) selects collision pairs statistically (no-time-counter scheme) with cross-sections from variable-hard-sphere or variable-soft-sphere models fitted to viscosity, exchanging internal energy by the Larsen–Borgnakke method and reacting by chemistry models; (5) samples macroscopic properties. Validity requires a time step below the mean collision time, cells smaller than about <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda/3</span>, and enough particles per cell. Results carry statistical noise that falls as <span class=\"mi\">1/\\sqrt{N}</span>. <em>Hybrid</em> CFD–DSMC methods couple the two across breakdown surfaces.</p>","40.10":"<p>Without collisions, surface forces follow from the flux of incoming molecules and the reflection law. High-speed satellites with diffuse reflection have <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} \\approx 2.0</span>–2.2, the value used in orbital decay (Chapter 30). The speed ratio is <span class=\"mi\">S = V/\\sqrt{2RT}</span>. For <span class=\"mi\">S \\gg 1</span>, the heat flux is <span class=\"mi\">q \\approx \\alpha \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\rho_{\\infty}V_{\\infty}^{3}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span> is the energy accommodation coefficient (0.8–1). This sets the fairing-jettison criterion (Chapter 31). Bridging functions interpolate coefficients between the free-molecular and continuum limits.</p>"}},"41":{"intro":"","secs":{"41.1":"<p>At a vehicle surface,</p><p><span class=\"md\">q_{conv} + q_{chem} + \\alpha_{r}q_{rad,in} = \\varepsilon\\sigma T_{w}^{4} + q_{cond} + q_{abl},</span></p><p>where the left side is incoming convective, chemical (catalytic recombination) and absorbed radiative flux. The right side is re-radiation, conduction into the structure and energy absorbed by ablation (Chapter 42). This balance is the boundary condition linking aerothermodynamics to thermal protection.</p>","41.2":"<p>For an equilibrium laminar boundary layer at an axisymmetric stagnation point, <strong>Fay and Riddell</strong> (1958) obtained:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">q_{w} = 0.763\\, Pr^{- 0.6}\\left( \\rho_{e}\\mu_{e} \\right)^{0.4}\\left( \\rho_{w}\\mu_{w} \\right)^{0.1}\\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{du_{e}}{dx} \\right)_{s}}\\,\\left( h_{0e} - h_{w} \\right)\\left\\lbrack 1 + \\left( Le^{0.52} - 1 \\right)\\frac{h_{D}}{h_{0e}} \\right\\rbrack</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">h_{D}</span> is the dissociation enthalpy at the boundary-layer edge (use <span class=\"mi\">Le^{0.63}</span> for a frozen boundary layer on a fully catalytic wall; replace 0.763 by 0.570 for a 2-D cylinder). Newtonian velocity gradient: <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\frac{du_{e}}{dx} \\right)_{s} = \\frac{1}{R_{n}}\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\left( p_{e} - p_{\\infty} \\right)}{\\rho_{e}}}</span>.</p></div><p>Because <span class=\"mi\">p_{e} \\approx \\rho_{\\infty}V_{\\infty}^{2}</span>, the result collapses to the engineering form</p><p><span class=\"md\">q_{s} = k\\sqrt{\\frac{\\rho_{\\infty}}{R_{n}}}V_{\\infty}^{3}\\quad\\text{(Sutton–Graves)},\\quad\\quad k = 1.7415 \\times 10^{- 4}\\ kg^{1/2}/m\\ \\text{(Earth air)},\\quad 1.9027 \\times 10^{- 4}\\ \\text{(Mars)},</span></p><p>in W/m². Two scalings drive vehicle design: heating rises as <span class=\"mi\">V^{3}</span>, and it falls as <span class=\"mi\">R_{n}^{- 1/2}</span>. Allen and Eggers’ insight (1953) was that a blunt body dissipates most of the entry energy into the shock-heated air rather than into the vehicle, which made ballistic entry survivable.</p>","41.3":"<p>Over blunt bodies, Lees’ laminar theory gives the heating distribution in terms of the local pressure and the integrated running length. On a hemisphere, heating falls off around the body roughly as the local pressure does. On sharp cones and wedges, use the reference-temperature method (Chapter 37) with the <em>Mangler transformation</em>: laminar heating on a cone is <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{3}</span> times that on a flat plate at the same edge conditions (about 1.1–1.2 times for turbulent flow). Turbulent heating scales roughly as <span class=\"mi\">\\rho^{0.8}V^{3}x^{- 0.2}</span> and is 3–8 times the laminar value at the same location. Transition location is therefore often the largest single uncertainty in thermal-protection sizing.</p>","41.4":"<p>At orbital speeds a large fraction of the boundary-layer edge enthalpy is chemical, stored in dissociated atoms. If the boundary layer is frozen (low density), atoms reach the wall intact, and the heat released depends on whether the surface catalyzes their recombination. The catalytic efficiency <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma_{w}</span> runs from 0 (non-catalytic) to 1 (fully catalytic). Non-catalytic surfaces, such as the Shuttle’s reaction-cured glass coating, can cut heating substantially relative to fully catalytic metals. A sudden change from a low-catalytic to a high-catalytic material produces a local <em>catalytic overshoot</em> in heating. Measured <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma_{w}</span> depends on temperature, pressure and surface state, and remains uncertain.</p>","41.5":"<p>Shock-layer radiation (Chapter 39) grows steeply with velocity, roughly as <span class=\"mi\">V^{8}</span> to <span class=\"mi\">V^{15}</span>, and is important for Earth entry above about 10 km/s. It is significant for lunar return (11 km/s), dominant for faster returns, and overwhelming for giant-planet entries such as the Galileo probe at about 47 km/s. Radiative heating <em>increases</em> with nose radius (more radiating gas), while convective heating decreases, so an optimum nose radius exists. Correlations take the form <span class=\"mi\">q_{r} = CR_{n}^{a}\\rho_{\\infty}^{b}f(V)</span> (Tauber–Sutton). Nonequilibrium overshoot, vacuum-ultraviolet emission and absorption by ablation products (“radiation blockage”) complicate prediction.</p>","41.6":"<p>When an oblique shock (from a forebody, wing or inlet ramp) strikes a bow shock near a surface, Edney’s six interaction types result. In the <strong>Type IV</strong> interaction, a supersonic jet forms between shear layers and impinges on the surface, producing a very narrow heating peak of 10 to 30 times the undisturbed stagnation value. In 1967 the X-15A-2 suffered severe local burn-through when the shock from a dummy ramjet hung beneath the aircraft interacted with its pylon. Cowl lips, wing and fin leading edges and struts must be designed to avoid or survive Type III and IV interactions. Sweeping a leading edge by <span class=\"mi\">\\Lambda</span> reduces stagnation-line heating roughly as <span class=\"mi\">\\cos^{n}\\Lambda</span> with <span class=\"mi\">n \\approx 1</span>–1.5.</p>","41.7":"<p>Gaps and steps between tiles, protruding gap fillers (which can trip transition), cavities (heating concentrated on the downstream lip), protuberances, hinge lines and control-surface interactions (shock–boundary-layer interaction, Chapter 37), corner vortices and base flows with plume interaction all create local heating peaks. They are often designed with empirical amplification factors validated by test.</p>","41.8":"<p>Reliable heat flux requires: a wall-normal spacing with cell Reynolds number <span class=\"mi\">Re_{cell} = \\rho_{w}a_{w}\\Delta y/\\mu_{w}</span> of order 1; grids aligned with the bow shock, to avoid spurious entropy and heating errors (including the “carbuncle”, Chapter 45); low-dissipation schemes near walls; appropriate thermochemical and catalytic boundary conditions; and turbulence and transition models. Validated predictions typically carry uncertainties of ±10–20% for laminar flow and considerably more for turbulent and interaction regions. Design margins and uncertainty quantification (Chapter 50) account for this. Fast engineering methods remain essential for coupling heating to trajectories.</p>","41.9":"<p>The <em>peak heat flux</em> <span class=\"mi\">q_{max}</span> sets surface temperature and therefore material selection. The <em>integrated heat load</em> <span class=\"mi\">Q = \\int q\\, dt</span> sets how much material is needed (ablator thickness or insulation depth). Steep entries give high <span class=\"mi\">q_{\\max}</span> and low <span class=\"mi\">Q</span>; shallow or lifting entries give low <span class=\"mi\">q_{\\max}</span> and high <span class=\"mi\">Q</span>. Trajectory and thermal-protection design must be optimized together (Chapter 43).</p>"}},"42":{"intro":"","secs":{"42.1":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Class</th> <th>Mechanism</th> <th>Examples</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Passive: heat sink</td> <td>Absorbs heat in its mass</td> <td>Early nose cones (copper, beryllium)</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Passive: hot structure</td> <td>Structure runs hot and re-radiates</td> <td>C/C or CMC control surfaces</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Passive: insulated structure</td> <td>High-emissivity, low-conductivity insulation over cool structure</td> <td>Shuttle tiles and blankets</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Semi-passive: ablator</td> <td>Absorbs heat by pyrolysis, blowing and surface reactions; consumed</td> <td>PICA, Avcoat, carbon-phenolic</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Semi-passive: heat pipe</td> <td>Spreads heat from a hot stagnation zone by phase change</td> <td>Liquid-metal leading-edge concepts</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Active</td> <td>Coolant carries heat away (transpiration, film, convective)</td> <td>Fuel-cooled scramjet walls</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Selection follows peak heat flux (material temperature limits), total heat load (thickness and mass), pressure and shear (mechanical erosion), reusability, mass and cost.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Beryllium, used in early heat-sink nose cones, is highly toxic as dust and can cause chronic beryllium disease. Carbon and ceramic fibers and dusts are respiratory hazards, and phenolic and epoxy resins and their solvents need chemical-hygiene controls.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Machining, sanding or burning thermal-protection and composite materials releases these hazards.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University materials-laboratory courses; OSHA and NIOSH guidance; institutional chemical-hygiene and respiratory-protection programs.</p></aside>","42.2":"<p>The Space Shuttle used silica-fiber tiles (LI-900: 144 kg/m³, coated with high-emissivity, low-catalycity reaction-cured glass) for surfaces up to about 1260 °C; flexible insulation blankets for cooler areas; and reinforced carbon–carbon (RCC) for the nose cap and wing leading edges, up to about 1650 °C. Later toughened systems (TUFI, TUFROC) improved impact resistance, and metallic TPS (superalloy honeycomb panels) offers robustness at lower temperatures. Reusable insulators are brittle and susceptible to impact damage; Columbia was lost when foam debris breached an RCC leading-edge panel. They also need gap and step control, waterproofing and inspection.</p>","42.3":"<p>Heated in depth, a charring ablator has three zones: virgin material; a <em>pyrolysis zone</em>, where the resin decomposes endothermically and releases gas; and a porous carbon <em>char</em>. The pyrolysis gases absorb heat as they percolate outward (transpiration) and then blow into the boundary layer, thickening it and reducing convective heating (<em>blockage</em>). At the surface, char recedes by oxidation (diffusion-limited at high temperature), sublimation (above about 3800 K for carbon) and mechanical removal (spallation, shear).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Blowing correction (film theory):</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{C_{H}}{C_{H0}} = \\frac{\\ln(1 + 2\\lambda B')}{2\\lambda B'}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">B' = {\\dot{m}}_{w}/\\left( \\rho_{e}u_{e}C_{H} \\right)</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda \\approx 0.5</span> (laminar) or 0.4 (turbulent). <strong>Surface energy balance</strong> for an ablator: <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{e}u_{e}C_{H}\\left( h_{r} - h_{w} \\right) + {\\dot{m}}_{c}h_{c} + {\\dot{m}}_{g}h_{g} - \\left( {\\dot{m}}_{c} + {\\dot{m}}_{g} \\right)h_{w} + \\alpha q_{rad} - \\varepsilon\\sigma T_{w}^{4} - q_{cond} = 0</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{m}}_{c}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{m}}_{g}</span> are the char and pyrolysis-gas mass fluxes. Surface thermochemistry is tabulated as <span class=\"mi\">B'_{c}\\left( B'_{g},T_{w},p \\right)</span> from equilibrium calculations.</p></div><p>For first sizing, the <em>effective heat of ablation</em> <span class=\"mi\">Q^{*} = q/\\dot{m}</span> converts a heat load into recession mass. Detailed design uses material-response codes (classically CMA and FIAT; the open-source PATO) coupled to the aerothermal environment.</p>","42.4":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Material</th> <th>Density (g/cm³)</th> <th>Heritage</th> <th>Character</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Carbon-phenolic</td> <td>≈ 1.4</td> <td>Pioneer Venus, Galileo</td> <td>Extreme heat flux and pressure; heavy</td> </tr> <tr> <td>PICA (phenolic-impregnated carbon ablator)</td> <td>≈ 0.25</td> <td>Stardust, Mars Science Laboratory</td> <td>Low density, high heat flux; brittle; tiled</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Avcoat (epoxy-novolac in honeycomb)</td> <td>≈ 0.5</td> <td>Apollo, Orion</td> <td>Moderate heat flux; monolithic or block forms</td> </tr> <tr> <td>SLA-561V (silicone-based)</td> <td>≈ 0.26</td> <td>Viking, Mars Pathfinder</td> <td>Low heat flux (Mars)</td> </tr> <tr> <td>3-D woven (e.g. HEEET)</td> <td>≈ 0.5–1.4 (dual layer)</td> <td>NASA development</td> <td>Tailorable for extreme entries</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Stardust’s PICA heat shield survived a 12.9 km/s Earth return in 2006, the fastest Earth entry of any human-made object. The Galileo probe entered Jupiter at about 47 km/s, and its carbon-phenolic shield lost a large fraction of its mass to ablation.</p>","42.5":"<ul> <li><strong>Nickel and cobalt superalloys:</strong> oxidation-resistant to about 1000 °C, but dense.</li> <li><strong>Refractory metals:</strong> niobium (C-103, with silicide coatings, to about 1370 °C), molybdenum, tantalum, tungsten. Strong when hot but oxidize catastrophically without coatings.</li> <li><strong>Carbon–carbon:</strong> retains strength above 2000 °C in inert gas, but oxidizes above about 450 °C and needs SiC conversion coatings and glass-forming sealants.</li> <li><strong>SiC-based CMCs (C/SiC, SiC/SiC):</strong> form protective silica scale (<em>passive oxidation</em>) to about 1500–1650 °C. At low oxygen partial pressure and high temperature, SiC instead forms volatile SiO (<em>active oxidation</em>) and recedes rapidly.</li> <li><strong>Ultra-high-temperature ceramics (ZrB₂, HfB₂ with SiC):</strong> melt above 3000 °C; form oxide scales plus borosilicate glass; candidates for sharp leading edges beyond 2000 °C. Their weaknesses are B₂O₃ evaporation, brittleness and thermal-shock sensitivity.</li> <li><strong>Oxide CMCs (alumina-based):</strong> immune to oxidation to roughly 1000–1200 °C.</li> </ul><p>Emissivity and catalycity are as important as strength, since they control the surface energy balance (Chapter 41).</p>","42.6":"<p>Solve 1-D transient conduction (Chapter 19) through the layered stack (outer TPS, bond, structure) with the surface energy balance as the boundary condition. Size the thickness so that the <em>bondline</em> stays below its limit, for example about 177 °C (350 °F) for aluminum structure, or the adhesive’s limit. Include <em>heat soak</em>: bondline temperature often peaks after heating ends. A first estimate of the required depth is the thermal penetration depth <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{\\alpha t}</span>. Margins cover heating uncertainty (±20–30%), material property scatter, recession uncertainty and manufacturing tolerances.</p>","42.7":"<p>Because <span class=\"mi\">q \\propto R_{n}^{- 1/2}</span>, small radii (for low drag and high L/D) mean severe heating. Options:</p><ul> <li><em>High-conductivity materials</em> (UHTCs, C/C with high-conductivity fibers) conduct heat aft to cooler, radiating surfaces.</li> <li><em>Heat pipes</em> with a liquid-metal working fluid (sodium or lithium) in a refractory envelope evaporate at the hot tip and condense aft, making the edge nearly isothermal. Start-up from a frozen state is the main difficulty.</li> <li><em>Transpiration cooling</em> through porous walls is very effective but risks clogging and coolant mass.</li> <li><em>Film</em> and <em>regenerative (fuel) cooling</em> are used for scramjet combustors (Chapter 44).</li> </ul>","42.8":"<p>RF-transparent windows (silica composites, silicon nitride) must keep stable dielectric properties when hot. Optical windows need thermal-shock resistance. Control surfaces need hot seals. Gaps must be filled and expansion accommodated without tripping transition (Chapter 41).</p>","42.9":"<p>Arc jets test material response at realistic heat flux and enthalpy for minutes, but at lower pressure and shear than flight and with a nonequilibrium free stream (Chapter 49). Lasers and quartz lamps provide radiant heating without chemistry. Flight instrumentation (such as the MEDLI sensor suites on Mars entries) measures in-depth temperatures, pressures and recession. Nondestructive evaluation (CT, ultrasound, thermography) screens manufacturing defects.</p>"}},"43":{"intro":"","secs":{"43.1":"<p>For planar entry over a non-rotating planet, with flight-path angle <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span> (negative descending) and bank angle <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma</span> (which tilts the lift vector):</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\dot{V} = - \\frac{D}{m} - g\\sin\\gamma,\\quad\\quad V\\dot{\\gamma} = \\frac{L}{m}\\cos\\sigma - \\left( g - \\frac{V^{2}}{r} \\right)\\cos\\gamma,\\quad\\quad\\dot{h} = V\\sin\\gamma.</span></p><p>The vehicle is characterized by its ballistic coefficient <span class=\"mi\">\\beta = m/\\left( C_{D}A \\right)</span> and its lift-to-drag ratio <span class=\"mi\">L/D</span>. Analytical work uses the exponential atmosphere <span class=\"mi\">\\rho = \\rho_{0}e^{- h/H}</span> (Earth: <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{0} = 1.225</span> kg/m³, <span class=\"mi\">H \\approx 7</span>–8 km).</p>","43.2":"<p>Neglect gravity relative to drag and hold <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span> constant. With <span class=\"mi\">dV/dt = - \\rho V^{2}/(2\\beta)</span> and <span class=\"mi\">dh = V\\sin\\gamma\\, dt</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">V = V_{E}\\exp\\left( - \\frac{\\rho H}{2\\beta\\sin|\\gamma|} \\right)</span>. <strong>Peak deceleration</strong> <span class=\"mi\">a_{\\max} = \\frac{V_{E}^{2}\\sin|\\gamma|}{2eH}</span> occurs at <span class=\"mi\">V = V_{E}e^{- 1/2} = 0.607V_{E}</span> and altitude <span class=\"mi\">h = H\\ln\\frac{\\rho_{0}H}{\\beta\\sin|\\gamma|}</span>; its value is <em>independent of β</em>. <strong>Peak stagnation heating</strong> (<span class=\"mi\">q \\propto \\sqrt{\\rho}V^{3}</span>) occurs at <span class=\"mi\">V = V_{E}e^{- 1/6} = 0.846V_{E}</span>. <strong>Heat load</strong> <span class=\"mi\">Q = kV_{E}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi\\beta H}{R_{n}\\sin|\\gamma|}}</span> (using the Sutton–Graves constant <span class=\"mi\">k</span>).</p></div><p>Steep entries bring high deceleration and high peak heating but a <em>smaller</em> heat load. Heavy (high-β) vehicles decelerate lower in the atmosphere, where denser air raises the heating. Peak deceleration is independent of β because a heavier body simply reaches the same velocity-loss profile deeper, where the density is proportionally higher.</p>","43.3":"<p>In <em>equilibrium glide</em> (<span class=\"mi\">\\gamma \\approx 0</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\gamma} \\approx 0</span>), lift balances weight minus centrifugal relief: <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^{2}C_{L}S/m = g\\left( 1 - V^{2}/gr \\right)</span>. Then</p><p><span class=\"md\">- \\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{g\\left( 1 - V^{2}/gr \\right)}{L/D},\\quad\\quad s = \\frac{r}{2}\\frac{L}{D}\\ln\\frac{1}{1 - V_{E}^{2}/(gr)}.</span></p><p>Deceleration is about <span class=\"mi\">g/(L/D)</span>: gentle (about 1.5g for the Shuttle). Peak heating is lower, but the long duration raises the heat load. Bank-angle modulation controls downrange, and <em>bank reversals</em> control crossrange (Chapter 46). The Shuttle’s crossrange requirement of about 2000 km drove its delta-wing design and hypersonic L/D of roughly 1–1.3.</p>","43.4":"<p>A lifting vehicle can rise out of the atmosphere after a first dip and re-enter later (<em>skip entry</em>), extending range for precise landing after lunar return. A lunar-return skip trajectory was demonstrated by the Chang’e 5-T1 test vehicle in 2014. The <em>entry corridor</em> lies between an <em>overshoot</em> boundary (too shallow: insufficient capture, skip-out) and an <em>undershoot</em> boundary (too steep: deceleration or heating limits exceeded). Its width grows with <span class=\"mi\">L/D</span> and sets the delivery accuracy that navigation must achieve.</p>","43.5":"<p>Capsules use large, blunt heat shields and trim at incidence through a CG offset (L/D about 0.25–0.4), steering by rolling the lift vector. Mars aeroshells use 60–70° sphere-cones.</p><ul> <li><strong>Mars:</strong> the atmosphere is about 1% of Earth’s surface pressure, so landers remain supersonic at low altitude. Supersonic parachutes (deployed near Mach 2) limit landed mass to about a tonne with traditional systems. Responses include the sky crane (Curiosity, Perseverance), supersonic retropropulsion (demonstrated in Earth’s upper atmosphere by returning boosters) and inflatable decelerators (LOFTID flight test, 2022).</li> <li><strong>Venus:</strong> dense, hot atmosphere; high entry heating.</li> <li><strong>Titan:</strong> thick atmosphere and low gravity make descent easy; Huygens landed in 2005.</li> <li><strong>Giant planets:</strong> extreme velocities and radiative heating (Galileo).</li> <li><strong>Earth return from deep space</strong> at 12–13 km/s: Stardust, Hayabusa, OSIRIS-REx.</li> </ul>","43.6":"<p>The Breguet range (Chapter 25), <span class=\"mi\">R = V\\, I_{sp}(L/D)\\ln\\left( W_{i}/W_{f} \\right)</span>, ranks cruise vehicles by the product <span class=\"mi\">V\\, I_{sp}\\,(L/D)</span>. A subsonic airliner (<span class=\"mi\">V \\approx 250</span> m/s, <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp} \\approx 6000</span> s on fuel, <span class=\"mi\">L/D \\approx 17</span>) achieves about <span class=\"mi\">2.6 \\times 10^{7}</span> m. A Mach 5–6 hydrocarbon-scramjet cruiser (<span class=\"mi\">V \\approx 1500</span>–1800 m/s, <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp} \\approx 1000</span>–1500 s, <span class=\"mi\">L/D \\approx 4</span>–5) reaches only about <span class=\"mi\">(0.6</span>–<span class=\"mi\">1.4) \\times 10^{7}</span> m. High speed is bought with range efficiency. Air-breathing vehicles also fly a constrained corridor of dynamic pressure (about 25–100 kPa) for engine operability and structural load (Chapter 44).</p>","43.7":"<p>A waverider is designed backward from a known shock flow field: the flow behind a planar oblique shock (Nonweiler’s caret wing, 1959), a conical shock (cone-derived), or a family of local conical flows (osculating cones). Its leading edges lie on the shock, so the high-pressure flow is contained beneath the vehicle and does not spill around the edges, giving high L/D at the design condition. Off-design performance, viscous drag, leading-edge blunting for heating and limited internal volume all reduce the ideal benefit. Integrating the forebody with a scramjet inlet is a natural extension (Chapter 44).</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Dual-use (scope note)</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> The flight mechanics of hypersonic gliding and cruise (§43.3, §43.6, §43.7) also underlie hypersonic weapons.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Weapon-specific material gives direct weapons uplift, is generally classified or controlled under ITAR and the MTCR, and is not needed for civil space, research or transport vehicles. The physics in this chapter is the same openly published physics used for capsules, orbiters and research aircraft.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Trajectory shaping to evade defenses, maneuvering re-entry-vehicle design, warhead or payload integration, targeting, and weapon accuracy budgets.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Open textbooks (Vinh, Busemann and Culp; Regan and Anandakrishnan; Appendix K) and university entry-flight-mechanics courses; NASA’s public technical reports. Weapon-specific work is done only inside authorized government and industry programs with clearances and export authorization. The policy and arms-control dimensions are studied through public academic and policy literature.</p></aside>","43.8":"<p>Flap effectiveness at hypersonic speeds is limited by separation and shock–boundary-layer interaction at large deflections. Reaction-control jets provide control at high altitude, where dynamic pressure is low. On the Shuttle’s first flight (1981), real-gas effects (lower effective γ) altered the pressure distribution on the expanding aft lower surface and produced an unpredicted nose-up pitching moment. Trim needed a body-flap deflection of about 16° instead of the predicted 7.5°. Cold wind tunnels (γ = 1.4) had missed it. Low-γ test gases (such as CF₄) and real-gas CFD now address such effects (Chapter 49).</p>","43.9":"<p>In body axes with velocity components <span class=\"mi\">(u,v,w)</span> and rates <span class=\"mi\">(p,q,r)</span>, the translational equations are <span class=\"mi\">m\\left( \\dot{u} + qw - rv \\right) = X - mg\\sin\\theta</span> and its analogs, and the rotational equations are Euler’s (Chapter 15) with aerodynamic moments <span class=\"mi\">L</span>, <span class=\"mi\">M</span>, <span class=\"mi\">N</span>. Aerodynamic forces and moments are expanded about a trim condition in <em>stability derivatives</em> such as <span class=\"mi\">C_{m\\alpha}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{mq}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{n\\beta}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{l\\beta}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{lp}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">C_{nr}</span>, plus control derivatives. For a symmetric vehicle, the linearized equations split into longitudinal and lateral-directional sets.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Longitudinal modes.</strong> <em>Short period</em>: a fast, usually well-damped oscillation in angle of attack and pitch rate, with <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{sp}^{2} \\approx - M_{\\alpha} + Z_{\\alpha}M_{q}/V</span> (dimensional derivatives). <em>Phugoid</em>: a slow exchange of kinetic and potential energy at nearly constant α; Lanchester’s approximation gives <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{ph} \\approx \\sqrt{2}\\, g/V</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\zeta_{ph} \\approx 1/\\left\\lbrack \\sqrt{2}(L/D) \\right\\rbrack</span>. <strong>Lateral-directional modes.</strong> <em>Roll subsidence</em> (time constant <span class=\"mi\">- 1/L_{p}</span>), <em>Dutch roll</em> (coupled yaw–roll oscillation set mainly by <span class=\"mi\">C_{n\\beta}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">C_{nr}</span>) and the slow <em>spiral</em> mode.</p></div><p>Hypersonic flight changes the balance. Aerodynamic damping scales with <span class=\"mi\">\\rho V</span>, so at high altitude every mode is lightly damped and reaction-control jets or stability augmentation must supply damping. At the high angles of attack of entry, directional departure is judged by the dynamic directional-stability parameter <span class=\"mi\">C_{n\\beta,dyn} = C_{n\\beta}\\cos\\alpha - \\left( I_{z}/I_{x} \\right)C_{l\\beta}\\sin\\alpha</span>, which must be positive. Ailerons can produce adverse yaw that reverses the roll response, so the Shuttle relied on yaw jets until dynamic pressure grew. Air-breathing hypersonic vehicles often have an unstable short period and a non-minimum-phase response to elevator (Chapter 46).</p>","43.10":"<p>Define the <em>energy height</em> <span class=\"mi\">E_{s} = h + V^{2}/(2g)</span> and the <em>specific excess power</em> <span class=\"mi\">P_{s} = dE_{s}/dt = (T - D)V/W</span>. Minimum-time climbs fly, at each energy level, the speed–altitude combination that maximizes <span class=\"mi\">P_{s}</span> (Rutowski’s energy-state approximation, formalized by Bryson). Minimum-fuel climbs maximize <span class=\"mi\">P_{s}/{\\dot{m}}_{f}</span>. Air-breathing hypersonic ascent usually follows a near-constant dynamic-pressure path (about 25–100 kPa) that balances engine operability against structural and heating limits, then transitions to rocket power or zooms (trading kinetic for potential energy) at the top of the corridor.</p>"}},"44":{"intro":"","secs":{"44.1":"<p>An air-breathing engine carries only fuel, so its fuel-specific impulse far exceeds a rocket’s. Hydrogen scramjets reach roughly 1000–4000 s (falling with Mach number), against about 450 s for the best rockets. The price is low thrust per unit frontal area and per unit engine weight, operation only within an atmospheric corridor (dynamic pressure about 25–100 kPa), and tight integration of engine and airframe.</p>","44.2":"<p>Uninstalled thrust: <span class=\"mi\">F = {\\dot{m}}_{a}\\left\\lbrack (1 + f)u_{e} - u_{0} \\right\\rbrack + \\left( p_{e} - p_{0} \\right)A_{e}</span>, with fuel–air ratio <span class=\"mi\">f</span>. Specific thrust is <span class=\"mi\">F/{\\dot{m}}_{a}</span>; fuel-specific impulse <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp} = F/\\left( {\\dot{m}}_{f}g_{0} \\right)</span>; thrust-specific fuel consumption <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{m}}_{f}/F</span>.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Efficiencies:</strong> thermal <span class=\"mi\">\\eta_{th}</span> = (rate of kinetic-energy gain of the flow)/(fuel energy rate); propulsive <span class=\"mi\">\\eta_{p} = Fu_{0}</span>/(rate of kinetic-energy gain) <span class=\"mi\">\\approx 2/\\left( 1 + u_{e}/u_{0} \\right)</span>; overall <span class=\"mi\">\\eta_{o} = \\eta_{th}\\eta_{p} = \\frac{Fu_{0}}{{\\dot{m}}_{f}h_{PR}}</span>. Hence <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp} = \\frac{\\eta_{o}h_{PR}}{g_{0}u_{0}}</span>: at fixed overall efficiency, Isp falls inversely with flight speed.</p></div>","44.3":"<p>Isentropic ram compression to near-zero velocity (<span class=\"mi\">T_{t0} = T_{0}\\tau_{r}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{r} = 1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M_{0}^{2}</span>), constant-pressure heat addition to <span class=\"mi\">T_{t4}</span>, and isentropic expansion to <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span> give <span class=\"mi\">M_{e} = M_{0}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">T_{e}/T_{0} = T_{t4}/T_{t0}</span>, hence:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{F}{{\\dot{m}}_{a}} = a_{0}M_{0}\\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{T_{t4}}{T_{t0}}} - 1 \\right)\\ (f \\ll 1),\\quad\\quad\\eta_{th} = 1 - \\frac{1}{\\tau_{r}}.</span></p><p>Ramjets work from about Mach 2 (enough ram compression) to about Mach 5–6. At higher speed, <span class=\"mi\">T_{t0}</span> approaches material and dissociation limits, and the total-pressure loss of the terminal normal shock in a real inlet grows rapidly.</p>","44.4":"<p>At Mach 7, <span class=\"mi\">T_{t} \\approx 2500</span> K. Decelerating the air to subsonic speed would raise its static temperature close to that value, near the onset of dissociation. Fuel added there would mostly dissociate products rather than raise temperature (Chapter 39), so thrust would collapse. Pressures and structural loads would also be extreme, and the normal-shock loss severe. Keeping the combustor flow supersonic (roughly Mach 2–3.5 at the combustor entrance for flight at Mach 6–10) holds static temperature and pressure within bounds. The price is a residence time of about a millisecond, in which fuel must mix and burn.</p>","44.5":"<p><strong>Inlet and forebody.</strong> The vehicle forebody and inlet ramps compress the air through a sequence of oblique shocks, ideally converging on the cowl lip at the design point (<em>shock-on-lip</em>). Compression is measured by the temperature ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\psi = T_{3}/T_{0}</span> and the <em>kinetic-energy efficiency</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\eta_{KE}</span> (the fraction of the kinetic energy that would be recovered by isentropic re-expansion to <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span>). Off-design operation causes spillage and loss of mass capture. Boundary layers on the long forebody are thick and may be tripped deliberately so that they resist separation in the inlet.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Starting and unstart.</strong> An internal-contraction inlet <em>starts</em> only if the normal shock that forms on start-up can pass through the throat. The <strong>Kantrowitz limit</strong> is <span class=\"mi\">A_{t}/A_{1} \\geq \\left( A^{*}/A \\right)|_{M_{2}}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">M_{2}</span> is the Mach number behind a normal shock at the entrance Mach number <span class=\"mi\">M_{1}</span>. <em>Unstart</em> is the sudden expulsion of the shock system ahead of the inlet, caused by excessive combustor back pressure (thermal choking), low Mach number or attitude excursions. It collapses thrust, produces violent transient loads and must be avoided or recovered by control.</p></div><p><strong>Isolator.</strong> A nearly constant-area duct between inlet and combustor contains a pre-combustion <em>shock train</em>, which lets the combustor raise its pressure without unstarting the inlet. The Waltrup–Billig correlation estimates the length needed for a pressure rise <span class=\"mi\">p_{2}/p_{1}</span> with incoming Mach <span class=\"mi\">M_{u}</span>, momentum thickness <span class=\"mi\">\\theta</span> and duct diameter <span class=\"mi\">D</span>: <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{L\\left( M_{u}^{2} - 1 \\right)Re_{\\theta}^{1/4}}{\\sqrt{\\theta D}} = 50\\left( \\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}} - 1 \\right) + 170\\left( \\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}} - 1 \\right)^{2}</span>, typically 8–12 duct heights.</p><p><strong>Combustor.</strong> Fuel enters by wall-normal jets (penetration governed by the jet-to-cross-flow momentum-flux ratio <span class=\"mi\">J = \\left( \\rho u^{2} \\right)_{j}/\\left( \\rho u^{2} \\right)_{\\infty}</span>), by ramps or struts that generate streamwise vorticity, or by combinations. Wall <em>cavities</em> provide recirculation zones for flame holding; pilots or plasma assist ignition. Heat release is limited by thermal choking (Chapter 24), so combustors diverge in area. In <em>dual-mode</em> operation, below about Mach 5–6, the shock train and heat release create a thermal throat with locally subsonic combustion (ramjet mode); above that, combustion is supersonic (scramjet mode). Hydrogen ignites quickly and is an excellent coolant but has very low density. Hydrocarbons (such as JP-7) are dense and storable but ignite slowly. Endothermic cracking of hydrocarbon fuels in cooling passages increases their heat-sink capacity.</p><p><strong>Nozzle.</strong> A <em>single-expansion ramp nozzle</em> uses the vehicle afterbody as one wall, expanding the flow externally beyond the cowl. Recombination may freeze (Chapter 39), and the nozzle’s force and moment couple strongly to vehicle trim.</p>","44.6":"<p>Heiser and Pratt’s method uses the <em>stream thrust function</em> <span class=\"mi\">S_{a} = u + p/(\\rho u) = u\\left( 1 + RT/u^{2} \\right)</span>, which turns the momentum equation of each component into a simple balance. The analysis steps are: (1) freestream (station 0) to combustor entrance (station 3) with specified <span class=\"mi\">\\psi</span> and efficiency; (2) combustion (constant-pressure or constant-area idealization) with fuel enthalpy <span class=\"mi\">h_{PR}</span>, combustion efficiency, wall friction and fuel momentum; (3) expansion to the exit with a nozzle efficiency; (4) thrust from the difference of exit and entrance stream thrusts.</p><div class=\"pit\"><p><strong>Net thrust is a small difference of large numbers.</strong> Inlet drag and nozzle thrust are each many times the net thrust at high Mach. A 1% error in a component efficiency can change net thrust by 10% or more, and the margin shrinks as Mach number rises. Bookkeeping of forces between “engine” and “airframe” must be explicit and consistent.</p></div>","44.7":"<p>No single air-breathing cycle runs from takeoff to orbit.</p><ul> <li><strong>Turbine-based (TBCC):</strong> a turbojet to about Mach 3–4, then a ramjet or scramjet; mode transition and inlet/nozzle sharing are the main challenges.</li> <li><strong>Rocket-based (RBCC):</strong> an embedded rocket acts as an ejector at low speed, then the duct runs as a ramjet and scramjet, then as a rocket again in vacuum.</li> <li><strong>Air-turborocket</strong>, <strong>liquid-air cycle</strong> (heavy) and <strong>precooled engines</strong>, in which a precooler drops inlet air from about 1000 °C to cryogenic temperatures in milliseconds. The SABRE precooler concept was ground-tested at Mach 5-equivalent temperatures.</li> </ul>","44.8":"<p>The forebody is the compression system and the afterbody is the nozzle, so aerodynamics and propulsion cannot be separated. Fuel volume (especially hydrogen) drives vehicle size. The fuel’s heat-sink capacity must exceed the vehicle’s integrated heat load. The operability envelope (angle of attack, dynamic pressure) constrains trajectories. Inlet behavior, thrust and pitching moment couple with elastic deformation, a stiff challenge for control (Chapter 46).</p>","44.9":"<p>Publicly documented research flights: HyShot II (2002), the first measured supersonic combustion in flight; NASA’s X-43A (2004), hydrogen-fueled, reaching Mach 6.8 and then Mach 9.7 for about 10 s of powered flight each; the X-51A (2013), hydrocarbon-fueled, sustaining about 210 s of scramjet operation near Mach 5.1; and the US–Australian HIFiRE program (2009–2018) on transition and scramjet physics. Flight remains the only complete validation (Chapter 49).</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Dual-use and export control (scope note)</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Scramjet and combined-cycle engine technology is used in hypersonic cruise missiles as well as in research and access-to-space vehicles. Hydrogen and high-pressure fuel systems also carry the physical hazards flagged in Chapters 27 and 54.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Detailed engine designs and test data are export-controlled (ITAR, EAR, MTCR) and give meaningful weapons uplift. The cycle analysis in this chapter is standard textbook material.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Engine-specific designs and dimensions, performance databases and test data beyond published summaries, and fuel or thermal-management designs for specific vehicles.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University hypersonic-propulsion courses and fundamental-research programs; Heiser and Pratt, Curran and Murthy, and Segal (Appendix K); NASA’s public technical reports and AIAA publications. Detailed engine work is done inside export-compliant organizations.</p></aside>","44.10":"<p>Pulse-detonation, rotating-detonation and oblique-detonation-wave engines seek pressure gain and shorter combustors by burning in detonation waves (Chapter 47).</p>"}},"45":{"intro":"","secs":{"45.1":"<p><em>Finite differences</em> approximate derivatives at points. <em>Finite volumes</em> integrate the conservation laws over cells, so conservation holds exactly in discrete form; most aerospace codes use them. <em>Finite elements</em> and <em>discontinuous Galerkin</em> methods use weak forms with local polynomials (DG offers high order on unstructured grids). A scheme must be <em>consistent</em> (truncation error → 0 as the grid is refined) and <em>stable</em> (errors stay bounded). By the <em>Lax equivalence theorem</em>, for well-posed linear problems, consistency plus stability is equivalent to convergence. The <em>modified equation</em> reveals what a scheme really solves. First-order upwinding of <span class=\"mi\">u_{t} + au_{x} = 0</span> actually solves <span class=\"mi\">u_{t} + au_{x} = \\nu_{num}u_{xx}</span> with numerical viscosity <span class=\"mi\">\\nu_{num} = \\frac{a\\Delta x}{2}(1 - CFL)</span>. Central schemes instead show dispersive errors, which appear as oscillations near discontinuities.</p>","45.2":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>CFL condition:</strong> the numerical domain of dependence must contain the physical one, so explicit schemes need <span class=\"mi\">CFL = \\left( |u| + a \\right)\\Delta t/\\Delta x \\leq C_{\\max}</span> (of order 1). <strong>Von Neumann analysis</strong> substitutes Fourier modes to find amplification factors: forward-time, centered-space differencing is unconditionally unstable for advection and stable for diffusion only if <span class=\"mi\">\\nu\\Delta t/\\Delta x^{2} \\leq \\frac{1}{2}</span>.</p></div>","45.3":"<p>Linear schemes that do not create new extrema (<em>monotone</em>) are at most first-order accurate. Sharp, oscillation-free shock capturing at higher order therefore requires <em>nonlinear</em> schemes, whose coefficients adapt to the solution through limiters.</p>","45.4":"<p>Treat the cell averages as piecewise-constant states. At each interface, solve the Riemann problem (shock, contact and rarefaction structure; Chapter 36) and use its flux. Approximate Riemann solvers:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Solver</th> <th>Idea</th> <th>Notes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Roe</td> <td>Exact solution of a locally linearized problem</td> <td>Sharp shocks and contacts; needs an entropy fix; prone to the carbuncle instability</td> </tr> <tr> <td>HLL / HLLE</td> <td>Two-wave approximation</td> <td>Very robust; smears contacts and shear layers</td> </tr> <tr> <td>HLLC</td> <td>Restores the contact wave</td> <td>Robust and accurate; widely used</td> </tr> <tr> <td>AUSM family (AUSM⁺, AUSM⁺-up)</td> <td>Splits convective and pressure fluxes</td> <td>Good at all speeds; low dissipation</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Flux-vector splitting (Steger–Warming, van Leer)</td> <td>Splits fluxes by the signs of the eigenvalues</td> <td>Robust; diffusive</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Central with artificial dissipation (JST)</td> <td>Central flux plus tuned dissipation</td> <td>Simple; needs careful tuning</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div>","45.5":"<p><em>MUSCL</em> reconstructs a piecewise-linear profile in each cell, with slopes limited (minmod, van Leer, van Albada, superbee) so that the scheme is total-variation diminishing (TVD). <em>ENO/WENO</em> schemes build high-order reconstructions from the smoothest of several stencils (fifth-order WENO is standard for shock–turbulence problems). For DNS and LES with shocks, low-dissipation central schemes are blended with shock-capturing near discontinuities using shock sensors (for example Ducros’).</p>","45.6":"<p>Explicit strong-stability-preserving Runge–Kutta methods (SSP-RK3) suit unsteady problems with small time steps. Implicit methods (backward Euler with linearized fluxes; LU-SGS; Newton–Krylov with GMRES and preconditioning) suit steady and stiff problems. Dual time stepping solves implicit unsteady problems. Local time stepping and multigrid accelerate convergence to steady state. Declare convergence only when residuals have dropped several orders of magnitude <em>and</em> integrated quantities (forces, heat flux) have stopped changing.</p>","45.7":"<p>Structured grids give the best quality and can align with shocks; unstructured grids handle complex geometry, with prismatic layers near walls; adaptive refinement (including adjoint-based adaptation) concentrates resolution where it matters. Characteristic boundary conditions specify as many quantities as there are incoming characteristics. Wall conditions include no-slip, isothermal or radiative-equilibrium temperature, catalysis and slip (Chapter 40).</p><div class=\"pit\"><p><strong>Heating-quality grids.</strong> Surface heat flux is far more grid-sensitive than pressure. Use wall spacing with <span class=\"mi\">Re_{cell} \\approx 1</span>, align the grid with the bow shock, avoid abrupt cell-size changes, and check that heating converges under refinement. The <em>carbuncle</em> (a spurious protrusion of the bow shock near the stagnation line with low-dissipation solvers) corrupts stagnation heating; cure it with HLLE- or AUSM-type fluxes, entropy fixes, rotated Riemann solvers or added dissipation at shocks.</p></div>","45.8":"<p>Viscous fluxes use central differences. Low-Reynolds-number turbulence models need <span class=\"mi\">y^{+} \\approx 1</span> at the first cell (Chapter 37). Compressible LES and DNS need high-order, low-dissipation numerics plus shock capturing, and careful inflow turbulence generation.</p>","45.9":"<p>Add species continuity and vibrational-energy equations (Chapter 40), mixture thermodynamics (NASA polynomials), transport models and catalytic wall conditions. The chemical source terms are stiff, so they are treated point-implicitly or fully implicitly while keeping species mass fractions positive. Radiation is usually loosely coupled. Publicly documented production codes include NASA’s LAURA, DPLR and FUN3D, the University of Minnesota’s US3D, SU2-NEMO, the University of Queensland’s Eilmer, and OpenFOAM-based solvers. NASA’s VULCAN-CFD is widely used for scramjets.</p>","45.10":"<ul> <li><strong>Code verification</strong> shows the equations are solved correctly. The <em>method of manufactured solutions</em> chooses an analytic solution, derives the source terms that make it exact, and confirms the theoretical order of accuracy under grid refinement.</li> <li><strong>Solution verification</strong> estimates numerical error in a specific result. With three grids refined by ratio <span class=\"mi\">r</span>, the observed order is <span class=\"mi\">p = ln\\left\\lbrack \\left( f_{3} - f_{2} \\right)/\\left( f_{2} - f_{1} \\right) \\right\\rbrack/lnr</span>, the Richardson estimate is <span class=\"mi\">f_{exact} \\approx f_{1} + \\left( f_{1} - f_{2} \\right)/\\left( r^{p} - 1 \\right)</span>, and Roache’s <em>Grid Convergence Index</em> is <span class=\"mi\">GCI = F_{s}|\\varepsilon|/\\left( r^{p} - 1 \\right)</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">F_{s} = 1.25</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span> the relative difference between the two finest grids.</li> <li><strong>Validation</strong> shows that the right equations were solved, by comparison with experiments of quantified uncertainty along a hierarchy from unit problems to complete systems (Chapters 49 and 52). Credibility frameworks such as NASA-STD-7009 formalize this.</li> </ul>","45.11":"<p>Domain decomposition with MPI distributes the grid; load balance and communication set the scaling. <em>Strong scaling</em> holds the problem fixed while adding processors; <em>weak scaling</em> grows the problem with them. GPUs accelerate memory-bandwidth-bound CFD kernels. I/O, data management and reproducibility (fixed seeds, recorded versions) become first-class concerns at scale.</p>"}},"46":{"intro":"","secs":{"46.1":"<p>For the linear model <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{x}_{k} = \\mathbf{F}\\mathbf{x}_{k - 1} + \\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{u}_{k} + \\mathbf{w}_{k}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{z}_{k} = \\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{x}_{k} + \\mathbf{v}_{k}</span>, with white Gaussian noises of covariance <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{Q}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{R}</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Predict:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">{\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}}^{-} = \\mathbf{F}\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}} + \\mathbf{Gu}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\mathbf{P}^{-} = \\mathbf{FP}\\mathbf{F}^{T} + \\mathbf{Q}</span>. <strong>Update:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{K} = \\mathbf{P}^{-}\\mathbf{H}^{T}\\left( \\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{P}^{-}\\mathbf{H}^{T} + \\mathbf{R} \\right)^{- 1}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}} = {\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}}^{-} + \\mathbf{K}\\left( \\mathbf{z} - \\mathbf{H}{\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}}^{-} \\right)</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\mathbf{P} = \\left( \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{KH} \\right)\\mathbf{P}^{-}\\left( \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{KH} \\right)^{T} + \\mathbf{KRK}^{T}</span> (Joseph form, numerically robust).</p></div><p>For linear-Gaussian systems the filter is the minimum-mean-square-error estimator, and its innovations <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{z} - \\mathbf{H}{\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}}^{-}</span> form a white sequence. Tuning <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{Q}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{R}</span> sets how much the filter trusts the model versus the measurements. Check consistency with the normalized estimation-error squared (in simulation, where truth is known) and the normalized innovation squared (in operation); both should follow their χ² distributions. States that are not observable cannot be estimated. Square-root and UD-factorized forms preserve positive-definite covariances in finite precision.</p>","46.2":"<p>The <em>extended Kalman filter</em> linearizes the dynamics and measurements about the current estimate using Jacobians. It is efficient but can diverge under strong nonlinearity or poor initialization. The <em>unscented Kalman filter</em> propagates deterministically chosen sigma points through the nonlinear functions, capturing the mean and covariance to second order without Jacobians. <em>Particle filters</em> represent arbitrary (including multimodal) distributions by weighted samples, at higher cost. Attitude uses the multiplicative EKF on an error quaternion (Chapter 32). <em>Smoothers</em> (Rauch–Tung–Striebel) use all data, past and future, for post-flight trajectory reconstruction.</p>","46.3":"<p>Inertial/GNSS integration is usually an <em>error-state</em> filter that estimates the INS errors and sensor biases (Chapter 34). Planetary landers add terrain-relative navigation, matching camera images to onboard maps (used by Mars 2020). Optical navigation uses stars, limbs and landmarks. Rendezvous uses lidar and cameras. During entry blackout, navigation is inertial only, so IMU quality and pre-blackout state accuracy set the error at exit.</p>","46.4":"<p>Minimize <span class=\"mi\">J = \\phi\\left( \\mathbf{x}\\left( t_{f} \\right),t_{f} \\right) + \\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{f}}L\\left( \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{u},t \\right)\\, dt</span> subject to <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{f}\\left( \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{u},t \\right)</span> and constraints. Form the Hamiltonian <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{H} = L + \\mathbf{\\lambda}^{T}\\mathbf{f}</span>.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Necessary conditions:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\partial\\mathcal{H/}\\partial\\mathbf{\\lambda}</span>; <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{\\lambda}} = - \\partial\\mathcal{H/}\\partial\\mathbf{x}</span>; <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u}^{*} = arg\\min_{\\mathbf{u} \\in U}\\mathcal{H}</span> (so <span class=\"mi\">\\partial\\mathcal{H/}\\partial\\mathbf{u} = 0</span> where unconstrained); transversality <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\lambda}\\left( t_{f} \\right) = \\partial\\phi/\\partial\\mathbf{x}</span> (plus multipliers for terminal constraints); <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{H}\\left( t_{f} \\right) = - \\partial\\phi/\\partial t_{f}</span> for free final time.</p></div><p>When <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{H}</span> is linear in a bounded control, the optimum is <em>bang-bang</em>, switching where the switching function changes sign, unless that function vanishes over an interval (a <em>singular arc</em>). Goddard’s maximum-altitude sounding-rocket problem has such an arc, on which thrust is throttled to balance drag. For powered flight over a flat Earth in vacuum with constant gravity, minimizing time gives costates that make the thrust direction obey the <em>linear tangent law</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\tan\\theta = (a - bt)/(c - dt)</span>, which reduces to <span class=\"mi\">\\tan\\theta = a - bt</span> for many problems (Chapter 31).</p>","46.5":"<p>For <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{Ax} + \\mathbf{Bu}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">J = \\int\\left( \\mathbf{x}^{T}\\mathbf{Qx} + \\mathbf{u}^{T}\\mathbf{Ru} \\right)\\, dt</span>, the optimal control is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u} = - \\mathbf{Kx}</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{K} = \\mathbf{R}^{- 1}\\mathbf{B}^{T}\\mathbf{P}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{P}</span> solves the algebraic Riccati equation</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\mathbf{A}^{T}\\mathbf{P} + \\mathbf{PA} - \\mathbf{PBR}^{- 1}\\mathbf{B}^{T}\\mathbf{P} + \\mathbf{Q} = 0.</span></p><p>Single-input LQR loops have guaranteed margins: gain margin from 1/2 to infinity and phase margin of at least 60°. LQG combines LQR with a Kalman filter (separation principle), but the combination has <em>no</em> guaranteed margins (Doyle, 1978). Robustness must be checked, and can be recovered by loop-transfer recovery.</p>","46.6":"<p><em>Indirect</em> methods solve the boundary-value problem defined by the necessary conditions, by single or multiple shooting. They are accurate but need good costate guesses. <em>Direct</em> methods transcribe the problem into a nonlinear program: collocation (trapezoidal, Hermite–Simpson) or pseudospectral methods (Legendre–Gauss–Radau, spectrally accurate for smooth problems), solved by SQP or interior-point codes with mesh refinement. Careful scaling of variables and constraints is essential.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Convex optimization for onboard guidance.</strong> Second-order cone programs are solved to global optimality in predictable time. <em>Lossless convexification</em> (Açıkmeşe and Ploen, 2007) relaxes the non-convex lower bound on thrust magnitude in powered descent and proves the relaxation exact, so fuel-optimal landing trajectories can be computed in real time. <em>Successive convexification</em> extends this to nonconvex problems such as aerodynamic descent.</p></div>","46.7":"<p>In the atmosphere, open-loop pitch programs respect load limits (Chapter 31). In vacuum, closed-loop guidance re-solves the steering problem repeatedly. The Saturn V’s Iterative Guidance Mode and the Shuttle’s Powered Explicit Guidance (PEG) were built on linear-tangent steering with predictor–corrector updates of burn time and steering parameters toward the target orbit, and could re-target after an engine failure.</p>","46.8":"<p>Entry vehicles steer by modulating bank angle: its magnitude controls the vertical lift (and hence downrange), and reversals of its sign control crossrange.</p><ul> <li><strong>Apollo</strong> tracked a reference trajectory with a range predictor and decided whether to skip.</li> <li><strong>Shuttle</strong> built a drag-acceleration-versus-velocity reference that respected heating, load and equilibrium-glide limits. It tracked the profile through the linearized drag dynamics and reversed bank when the heading error exceeded a velocity-dependent deadband.</li> <li><strong>Numerical predictor–correctors</strong> integrate the remaining trajectory onboard for a candidate bank profile and correct it to reach the target. They are robust to large dispersions.</li> </ul><p>Guided entry let Mars Science Laboratory land within an ellipse of about 20 km by 7 km, far smaller than earlier unguided Mars landings.</p>","46.9":"<p>Apollo’s lunar module used polynomial (quadratic-in-time acceleration) guidance to meet terminal position, velocity and acceleration targets. Zero-effort-miss/zero-effort-velocity feedback guidance commands, in uniform gravity,</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\mathbf{a}_{cmd} = \\frac{6}{t_{go}^{2}}\\mathbf{ZEM} - \\frac{2}{t_{go}}\\mathbf{ZEV},</span></p><p>where ZEM and ZEV are the terminal position and velocity errors predicted if no further control were applied. This is energy-optimal for that model. Convex-optimization guidance with large diverts (G-FOLD) was flight-tested on terrestrial testbeds in 2012–2013. Reusable booster landing guidance has been publicly described as convex-optimization-based.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Dual-use (scope note)</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Guidance, navigation and control theory is shared by spacecraft and weapons.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Weapon-guidance functions give direct targeting uplift and are controlled. This chapter covers the guidance problems of civil spaceflight (ascent, entry, landing, rendezvous) and the estimation and control theory common to all fields.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Intercept and homing guidance laws, target tracking and engagement, terminal guidance for strike, and accuracy optimization for weapons.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Graduate courses in optimal control, estimation and robust control, and the textbooks in Appendix K; the open sessions and archival papers of the AIAA Guidance, Navigation, and Control Conference. Weapon-specific guidance is studied only within authorized programs.</p></aside>","46.10":"<p>Uncertainty may be parametric or unstructured. <em>H∞</em> synthesis minimizes worst-case gain from disturbances to errors, and <em>μ-analysis</em> certifies robustness to structured uncertainty. Across a flight envelope, controllers are <em>gain-scheduled</em> (or designed as linear parameter-varying systems). <em>Nonlinear dynamic inversion</em> cancels known dynamics; its <em>incremental</em> form (INDI) uses measured accelerations and is robust to model error. <em>Adaptive</em> control (model-reference, L1) adjusts gains online but complicates certification.</p><p>Air-breathing hypersonic vehicles are especially demanding. Their short-period mode is often unstable. The elevator-to-flight-path response is non-minimum-phase (the vehicle initially moves the wrong way). Thrust depends on angle of attack through the inlet. Low-frequency fuselage bending changes inlet conditions. Actuators are rate-limited, and unstart must be avoided as a hard constraint. Validation combines linear margin analysis, nonlinear Monte Carlo simulation and, increasingly, formal methods.</p>","46.11":"<p>Onboard planning, fault-tolerant re-planning and learned policies (reinforcement learning, neural-network controllers) are research frontiers. Their safe use relies on runtime assurance: a verified simple controller that takes over when a monitor detects unsafe behavior (simplex architecture), and on formal verification of neural networks (Chapter 53).</p>"}},"47":{"intro":"","secs":{"47.1":"<p>Non-premixed systems are organized by the <em>mixture fraction</em> <span class=\"mi\">Z</span>, a conserved scalar; premixed systems by a <em>progress variable</em> <span class=\"mi\">c</span>. Two dimensionless groups locate a flame’s regime: the Damköhler number <span class=\"mi\">Da = \\tau_{flow}/\\tau_{chem}</span>, and the Karlovitz number <span class=\"mi\">Ka = \\left( \\delta_{L}/\\eta \\right)^{2}</span>, which compares the laminar flame thickness with the Kolmogorov scale.</p>","47.2":"<p>On the Borghi–Peters diagram, premixed flames are <em>wrinkled or corrugated flamelets</em> (<span class=\"mi\">Ka &lt; 1</span>), <em>thin reaction zones</em> (<span class=\"mi\">1 &lt; Ka &lt; 100</span>, where small eddies enter the preheat zone) or <em>broken reaction zones</em> (<span class=\"mi\">Ka &gt; 100</span>). Models include eddy-dissipation concepts (mixing-limited), flamelet and flamelet/progress-variable methods (chemistry tabulated against <span class=\"mi\">Z</span>, scalar dissipation and <span class=\"mi\">c</span>), transported probability-density-function methods (exact chemistry closure, modeled mixing), conditional moment closure, and thickened-flame LES. Supersonic combustion adds compressibility, shock–flame interaction and autoignition-dominated stabilization, where flamelet assumptions often fail.</p>","47.3":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Compressibility suppresses mixing.</strong> For a shear layer between streams of speeds <span class=\"mi\">U_{1}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">U_{2}</span> (equal γ), the convective Mach number is <span class=\"mi\">M_{c} = \\left( U_{1} - U_{2} \\right)/\\left( a_{1} + a_{2} \\right)</span>. The growth rate, normalized by its incompressible value, falls to about 0.2–0.3 for <span class=\"mi\">M_{c} \\gtrsim 1</span> (Papamoschou and Roshko). Scramjets therefore need mixing enhancement: streamwise vortices from ramps and struts, shock-induced (baroclinic) mixing and pulsed or angled jets.</p></div>","47.4":"<p>The ignition delay <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{ign}</span> depends exponentially on temperature (and on pressure through chain-branching kinetics, Chapter 17). For hydrogen–air near 1000 K it is a fraction of a millisecond, much longer at lower temperatures, and longer still for hydrocarbons. The induction length <span class=\"mi\">u\\tau_{ign}</span> must fit inside the combustor. Cavity flame holders lengthen residence time; radicals generated in hot regions (“radical farming”) can trigger ignition downstream; plasma assistance shortens delays.</p>","47.5":"<p>Above the critical pressure, surface tension vanishes and cryogenic propellants enter as dense, gas-like jets with steep density gradients rather than as droplets. Properties change sharply across a pseudo-boiling line. Real-gas equations of state (Peng–Robinson, Soave–Redlich–Kwong) and real-fluid transport are required. Staged-combustion engines at 20–30 MPa exceed the critical pressures of oxygen (5.04 MPa) and methane (4.60 MPa), so this regime is the norm in modern main chambers.</p>","47.6":"<p>Liquid breakup is governed by the Weber number <span class=\"mi\">We = \\rho_{g}u_{rel}^{2}d/\\sigma</span> (aerodynamic force over surface tension) and the Ohnesorge number <span class=\"mi\">Oh = \\mu_{l}/\\sqrt{\\rho_{l}\\sigma d}</span> (viscous damping). Rising <span class=\"mi\">We</span> moves breakup from bag to stripping to catastrophic regimes. Primary atomization of jets and sheets is followed by secondary droplet breakup. Spray quality is summarized by the Sauter mean diameter <span class=\"mi\">d_{32}</span>; vaporization follows the <span class=\"mi\">d^{2}</span> law (Chapter 17).</p>","47.7":"<p>With unsteady heat release <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{q}'</span>, the acoustic pressure obeys</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{1}{a^{2}}\\frac{\\partial^{2}p'}{\\partial t^{2}} - \\nabla^{2}p' = \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{a^{2}}\\frac{\\partial\\dot{q}'}{\\partial t}.</span></p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Rayleigh index</strong> <span class=\"mi\">R = \\oint\\int_{V}^{}p'\\dot{q}'\\, dV\\, dt</span>: oscillations grow if <span class=\"mi\">R &gt; 0</span> exceeds acoustic losses (nozzle, walls, viscosity, radiation). <strong>Crocco’s n–τ model:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{q}'/\\bar{\\dot{q}} = n\\, p'(t - \\tau)/\\bar{p}</span>. A mode of frequency <span class=\"mi\">f</span> is driven when the phase lag <span class=\"mi\">2\\pi f\\tau</span> lies within ±90° of a multiple of 360°.</p></div><p>Linear stability is analyzed with Helmholtz solvers or acoustic network models using measured or computed flame transfer functions. Instabilities may be <em>linearly unstable</em> (growing from noise) or <em>triggered</em> (stable to small but unstable to large perturbations, a subcritical bifurcation to a limit cycle). This is why bomb tests are used (Chapter 27). Mitigation: baffles, acoustic cavities, injector impedance tuning and added damping. Quantitative prediction remains a frontier, increasingly pursued with LES.</p>","47.8":"<p>The Hugoniot curve of burned states (shifted by the heat release <span class=\"mi\">q</span>) and the Rayleigh line from the initial state intersect where mass, momentum and energy are all conserved. The minimum-speed self-sustaining solution is the <strong>Chapman–Jouguet</strong> (CJ) detonation, where the Rayleigh line is tangent to the Hugoniot and the products leave at their local sound speed, the reacting analog of thermal choking (Chapter 24). For strong detonations, <span class=\"mi\">D_{CJ} \\approx \\sqrt{2\\left( \\gamma^{2} - 1 \\right)q}</span>. The <strong>ZND</strong> structure is a leading shock (the von Neumann spike, at about twice the CJ pressure), an induction zone, then a reaction zone. Real detonations are <em>cellular</em>, with transverse waves and triple points. Cell width <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda</span> measures detonability: about 1–2 mm for stoichiometric H₂–O₂ at 1 atm, about 1 cm for H₂–air, and tens of centimeters for methane–air. Detonations cannot pass through tubes narrower than a critical diameter of about <span class=\"mi\">13\\lambda</span>. Deflagration-to-detonation transition (DDT) needs a run-up distance, which is a central safety concern.</p>","47.9":"<p>Constant-volume and detonative heat addition (Humphrey or ZND cycles) are more efficient than constant-pressure (Brayton) heat addition (Chapter 16). A <em>pulse-detonation engine</em> fills, detonates and blows down cyclically, which limits its frequency. A <em>rotating detonation engine</em> (RDE) sustains one or more detonation waves running continuously around an annulus fed with propellant. Waves typically run somewhat below CJ speed, and the number of waves depends on injection and geometry. The difficulties are injector recovery and coupling, parasitic deflagration, high heat flux, and actually realizing net pressure gain. Rocket RDEs have been demonstrated in ground tests in the early 2020s. <em>Oblique detonation wave engines</em> stabilize a standing detonation on a wedge in hypersonic flow, promising very short combustors if the wave can be stabilized.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Detonation experiments produce extreme pressures, intense noise and fragment hazards. Test cells need blast protection, remote operation and strict procedures. Some experiments use regulated explosive initiators.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Rotating detonation engines and detonation research are legitimate propulsion science, but their hardware fails violently.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Initiator and explosive details.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University and government combustion laboratories with formal safety programs; graduate research positions; the Combustion Institute and AIAA propulsion conferences.</p></aside>"}},"48":{"intro":"","secs":{"48.1":"<p>A plasma is an ionized gas that is <em>quasi-neutral</em> over scales larger than the <strong>Debye length</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda_{D} = \\sqrt{\\varepsilon_{0}kT_{e}/\\left( n_{e}e^{2} \\right)}</span>. Electrons oscillate collectively at the <strong>plasma frequency</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{pe} = \\sqrt{n_{e}e^{2}/\\left( \\varepsilon_{0}m_{e} \\right)}</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mi\">f_{pe} \\approx 8.98\\sqrt{n_{e}}</span> Hz with <span class=\"mi\">n_{e}</span> in m⁻³. In a magnetic field, electrons gyrate at <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{ce} = eB/m_{e}</span> with Larmor radius <span class=\"mi\">r_{L} = m_{e}v_{\\bot}/(eB)</span>. The <strong>Hall parameter</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\beta_{e} = \\omega_{ce}/\\nu_{e}</span> (gyrofrequency over collision frequency) tells whether electrons are magnetized (<span class=\"mi\">\\beta_{e} &gt; 1</span>). The electrical conductivity is <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = n_{e}e^{2}/\\left( m_{e}\\nu_{e} \\right)</span>; in hypersonic shock layers it is typically tens to thousands of S/m.</p>","48.2":"<p>Orbital entry produces shock-layer electron densities of <span class=\"mi\">10^{17}</span>–<span class=\"mi\">10^{20}</span> m⁻³.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Blackout criterion:</strong> radio waves with <span class=\"mi\">f &lt; f_{pe}</span> are reflected or strongly attenuated. The critical density is <span class=\"mi\">n_{e,crit} = (f/8.98)^{2}</span> m⁻³: about <span class=\"mi\">6 \\times 10^{16}</span> m⁻³ at S-band (2.2 GHz) and <span class=\"mi\">9 \\times 10^{17}</span> m⁻³ at X-band (8.4 GHz). Collisions add attenuation even below critical density.</p></div><p>Mitigation: higher frequencies (Ka-band and above); antennas in the base or wake, where densities are lower; relay geometry (the Shuttle communicated upward through its wake to relay satellites); slender, sharp shapes, which produce fewer electrons; injection of electrophilic quenchants; and applied magnetic fields that open “magnetic windows”. Navigation through blackout is inertial (Chapter 46).</p>","48.3":"<p>The magnetic field evolves by the induction equation <span class=\"mi\">\\partial\\mathbf{B}/\\partial t = \\nabla \\times \\left( \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{B} \\right) + \\frac{1}{\\mu_{0}\\sigma}\\nabla^{2}\\mathbf{B}</span>. Two dimensionless groups govern MHD flow. The <strong>magnetic Reynolds number</strong> <span class=\"mi\">Rm = \\mu_{0}\\sigma UL</span> (advection versus diffusion of the field) is small in most aerospace plasmas, so applied fields are barely distorted. The <strong>interaction (Stuart) parameter</strong> <span class=\"mi\">Q = \\sigma B^{2}L/(\\rho U)</span> compares the Lorentz force <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{j} \\times \\mathbf{B}</span> with inertia. Hall currents and ion slip complicate real devices. Hypersonic applications include magnetoaerodynamic heat-flux and drag control during entry, MHD “energy bypass” for scramjets (extracting flow energy upstream of the combustor with an MHD generator and returning it downstream with an accelerator), and onboard power generation. The obstacles are air’s low conductivity (requiring seeding or nonequilibrium ionization) and magnet mass.</p>","48.4":"<p>Electric propulsion separates the energy source (electric power <span class=\"mi\">P</span>) from the propellant.</p><div class=\"key\"><p>Jet power <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{1}{2}\\dot{m}v_{e}^{2} = \\frac{1}{2}Fv_{e} = \\eta P</span>. Hence the <strong>thrust-to-power ratio</strong> is <span class=\"mi\">F/P = 2\\eta/v_{e}</span>: at fixed power, raising exhaust velocity lowers thrust proportionally. With power-system specific mass <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span> (kg/kW) and burn time <span class=\"mi\">t_{b}</span>, the characteristic velocity <span class=\"mi\">v_{ch} = \\sqrt{2\\eta t_{b}/\\alpha}</span> sets the scale of the optimal exhaust velocity (Stuhlinger); higher or lower <span class=\"mi\">v_{e}</span> wastes mass on propellant or on power plant.</p></div><p>Thrusts are millinewtons to newtons, so maneuvers last weeks to years and trajectories are spirals (Chapter 30).</p>","48.5":"<p><em>Resistojets</em> heat propellant over a resistive element (hydrazine-augmented versions about 300 s). <em>Arcjets</em> pass the propellant through an electric arc (about 500–600 s with hydrazine; more with hydrogen or ammonia). Electrode erosion and frozen-flow losses (energy left in dissociation, Chapter 39) limit efficiency.</p>","48.6":"<p><strong>Gridded ion thrusters</strong> ionize propellant (by DC discharge, RF or microwave) and accelerate ions through grids at beam voltage <span class=\"mi\">V_{b}</span>: <span class=\"mi\">v_{e} = \\sqrt{2qV_{b}/m_{i}}</span>. Xenon at 1100 V gives about 40 km/s. Current density is limited by space charge, as given by the <strong>Child–Langmuir law</strong></p><p><span class=\"md\">j = \\frac{4\\varepsilon_{0}}{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{2q}{m_{i}}}\\frac{V^{3/2}}{d^{2}},</span></p><p>so thrust density is low. A neutralizer cathode keeps the spacecraft from charging. Grid erosion by charge-exchange ions limits life. NSTAR (Deep Space 1, Dawn) delivered about 3100 s and NEXT about 4100 s.</p><p><strong>Hall thrusters</strong> use a radial magnetic field to trap electrons in an azimuthal Hall current. The axial electric field then accelerates ions through a quasi-neutral plasma, so there is no space-charge limit and thrust density is higher. They deliver 1500–3000 s at 50–60% efficiency. <em>Magnetic shielding</em> keeps energetic ions away from the channel walls and greatly extends life. Hall thrusters dominate commercial orbit raising and station keeping, increasingly with krypton or argon in place of costly xenon. Electrospray and field-emission thrusters provide micronewton precision for small spacecraft.</p>","48.7":"<p><em>Magnetoplasmadynamic (MPD)</em> thrusters accelerate plasma with the self-field <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{j} \\times \\mathbf{B}</span> force at very high power (hundreds of kW to MW), limited by electrode erosion and an “onset” instability. <em>Pulsed plasma thrusters</em> ablate a solid propellant (typically PTFE) in microsecond pulses. <em>Pulsed inductive</em> thrusters avoid electrodes altogether. <em>VASIMR</em> combines helicon ionization, ion-cyclotron heating and a magnetic nozzle; it has been tested on the ground but not flown. Magnetic nozzles must detach the plasma from the field lines to produce thrust.</p>","48.8":"<p>Solar arrays supply 1361 W/m² at 1 AU, falling as <span class=\"mi\">1/r^{2}</span>, at about 30% cell efficiency and more than 100 W/kg for modern arrays. Radioisotope generators (Pu-238) supply a few W/kg; the MMRTG provides about 110 W at the start of its life. Fission systems at 1–10 kWe were demonstrated by the KRUSTY ground test (2018). At high power, waste-heat radiators dominate the mass (Chapter 51).</p>","48.9":"<p>Vacuum facilities alter thruster behavior through background pressure (ingested background gas, facility-enhanced thrust and electrical coupling to chamber walls). Lifetime is qualified by long wear tests (thousands of hours) combined with physics-based erosion models. Plumes can sputter, contaminate and charge spacecraft surfaces.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Electric-propulsion work involves voltages of hundreds to thousands of volts, high-power RF, large vacuum chambers and high-pressure gas bottles.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> High-voltage and RF hazards can kill without visible warning.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University plasma and electric-propulsion laboratories with electrical-safety training (high-voltage procedures, lockout/tagout); JPL’s free text by Goebel and Katz and NASA public reports for the theory.</p></aside>"}},"49":{"intro":"","secs":{"49.1":"<p>Full simulation of hypersonic flight requires matching Mach number, Reynolds number, total enthalpy (<span class=\"mi\">h_{0} \\approx V^{2}/2</span>), the binary-scaling parameter <span class=\"mi\">\\rho L</span>, the wall-to-total temperature ratio, gas composition and chemistry (Damköhler number) and the free-stream disturbance (noise) environment. No ground facility matches all of these at once. Cold hypersonic tunnels match Mach and Reynolds numbers but not enthalpy. High-enthalpy impulse facilities match velocity and <span class=\"mi\">\\rho L</span> but often at lower Mach number, with nonequilibrium free streams and noise. Arc jets match heat flux and enthalpy for minutes but not geometry, pressure or shear. Programs therefore combine <em>partial simulations</em>, bridged by validated CFD and confirmed by flight.</p>","49.2":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Facility</th> <th>Principle</th> <th>Test time</th> <th>Strengths</th> <th>Limits</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Conventional hypersonic tunnel (Mach 5–14)</td> <td>Heated air or N₂ blowdown</td> <td>Seconds to minutes</td> <td>Perfect-gas aerodynamics; Re effects; heating trends</td> <td>No real-gas chemistry; noisy walls</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Ludwieg tube (incl. quiet tunnels)</td> <td>Expansion wave in a charge tube</td> <td>~100 ms</td> <td>Quiet versions reproduce flight-like disturbance levels</td> <td>Low enthalpy</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Reflected-shock tunnel (e.g. LENS, T5, HEG, HIEST, T4)</td> <td>Shock-heated stagnant reservoir expanded through a nozzle</td> <td>~1–10 ms</td> <td>High enthalpy (to ~20+ MJ/kg); scramjets; heating</td> <td>Short time; driver-gas contamination; frozen nozzle flow</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Expansion tube or tunnel (e.g. X2, X3)</td> <td>Unsteady expansion adds energy without stagnating the gas</td> <td>10s–100s of μs</td> <td>Superorbital velocities; radiation</td> <td>Very short time; small core</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Arc jet / plasma tunnel</td> <td>Continuous electric-arc heating</td> <td>Minutes</td> <td>TPS material response at flight heat flux</td> <td>Low pressure and shear; nonequilibrium free stream</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Vitiated (combustion-heated) tunnel</td> <td>Burn fuel in air and replenish oxygen</td> <td>Seconds to minutes</td> <td>Large-scale scramjet engine tests</td> <td>Contaminants (H₂O, CO₂, radicals) alter ignition and combustion</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Ballistic range</td> <td>Model fired from a light-gas gun into still gas</td> <td>Free flight</td> <td>True free flight; stability; transition</td> <td>Small models; limited instrumentation</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Shock tubes store large energies behind bursting diaphragms. Arc jets draw megawatts of electrical power. Class 4 laser diagnostics can blind instantly. Some diagnostics use toxic gases (NO, CO). High-pressure gas and oxygen systems are everywhere in test facilities.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> These hazards are why test facilities run under formal safety programs with trained, authorized operators.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Facility-specific training under an institutional safety program; laser-safety training to the ANSI Z136 standard with a laser safety officer; graduate research assistantships and internships at university and government test facilities.</p></aside>","49.3":"<p>For a perfect gas, an incident shock of Mach number <span class=\"mi\">M_{s}</span> reflected from the closed end of the tube produces the reservoir state 5:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{T_{5}}{T_{1}} = \\frac{\\left\\lbrack 2(\\gamma - 1)M_{s}^{2} + (3 - \\gamma) \\right\\rbrack\\left\\lbrack (3\\gamma - 1)M_{s}^{2} - 2(\\gamma - 1) \\right\\rbrack}{(\\gamma + 1)^{2}M_{s}^{2}},\\quad\\quad\\frac{p_{5}}{p_{1}} = \\frac{2\\gamma M_{s}^{2} - (\\gamma - 1)}{\\gamma + 1} \\cdot \\frac{(3\\gamma - 1)M_{s}^{2} - 2(\\gamma - 1)}{(\\gamma - 1)M_{s}^{2} + 2}.</span></p><p>Real-gas effects lower <span class=\"mi\">T_{5}</span> substantially at high <span class=\"mi\">M_{s}</span>. The stagnation enthalpy is <span class=\"mi\">h_{0} \\approx h_{5}</span>. Useful test time ends when driver gas reaches the nozzle; tailored-interface operation extends it (Chapter 36).</p>","49.4":"<ul> <li><strong>Pressure:</strong> piezoresistive transducers for static and dynamic pressure; piezoelectric sensors with MHz-class resonance for second-mode instability waves (Chapter 37); pressure-sensitive paint for global maps.</li> <li><strong>Heat flux:</strong> thin-film resistance gauges (a platinum film on a low-conductivity substrate) respond in microseconds; semi-infinite conduction theory (Chapter 12), as in the Cook–Felderman method, converts the measured surface-temperature history to heat flux. Coaxial thermocouples are robust with millisecond response. Schmidt–Boelter and Gardon gauges measure steady flux; slug calorimeters are used in arc jets. Infrared thermography, temperature-sensitive paints and phosphor thermography give global surface temperatures, converted to heat flux by inverse conduction.</li> <li><strong>Force:</strong> strain-gauge balances (longer tests), accelerometer balances, stress-wave force balances (millisecond facilities), free-flight tracking.</li> <li><strong>Flow field:</strong> schlieren and shadowgraph imaging respond to first and second spatial derivatives of refractive index, which relates to density by the Gladstone–Dale law <span class=\"mi\">n - 1 = K\\rho</span> (<span class=\"mi\">K \\approx 2.26 \\times 10^{- 4}</span> m³/kg for air); focusing schlieren isolates a plane. Interferometry gives quantitative density. Planar laser-induced fluorescence (NO, OH) maps species and temperature. Tunable diode-laser absorption gives path-averaged H₂O temperature and concentration, hence scramjet combustion efficiency. CARS measures temperature and species; Rayleigh and Raman scattering measure density and composition; particle image velocimetry suffers particle lag across shocks; molecular tagging (for example FLEET) measures velocity without seeding; emission spectroscopy characterizes radiation.</li> </ul>","49.5":"<p>Separate random and systematic uncertainties (ASME PTC 19.1; Coleman and Steele) and propagate them analytically or by Monte Carlo (Chapter 13). Characterize the facility itself: calibration rakes, free-stream noise and uniformity, and computed nonequilibrium nozzle flow, since free-stream uncertainty often dominates. Demonstrate repeatability, and replicate key conditions.</p>","49.6":"<p>Flight is the only complete simulation. Approaches include sounding rockets, which give minutes at hypersonic speed at modest cost (HIFiRE, BOLT); air-launched boosters (X-43A rode a modified Pegasus booster dropped from a B-52); instrumented entry vehicles (MEDLI on Mars landers); and reusable testbeds. The historical record includes the X-15 (1959–1968; Mach 6.7 and 108 km altitude), Project Fire II (1965; radiative heating at 11.35 km/s), RAM-C (1960s; plasma blackout data), the Shuttle orbiter experiments, HyShot, the X-43A and the X-51A. Flight data are reconstructed into a best-estimated trajectory by smoothing (Chapter 46) and compared with blind predictions.</p>","49.7":"<p>Validation experiments differ from discovery experiments. They are <em>co-designed</em> with modelers, measure every boundary and initial condition the model needs, quantify all uncertainties, and are compared with <em>blind</em> predictions. They are organized as a building-block hierarchy: unit physics, then benchmark cases, then subsystems, then the complete system (Chapters 45 and 52).</p>"}},"50":{"intro":"","secs":{"50.1":"<p>Minimize <span class=\"mi\">f\\left( \\mathbf{x} \\right)</span> subject to <span class=\"mi\">g_{j}\\left( \\mathbf{x} \\right) \\leq 0</span>, <span class=\"mi\">h_{k}\\left( \\mathbf{x} \\right) = 0</span> and bounds. With several objectives, the answer is a <em>Pareto front</em> of non-dominated designs. Weighted sums miss non-convex parts of the front; ε-constraint methods and evolutionary algorithms (such as NSGA-II) trace it fully. Gradient-based methods scale to thousands of variables; gradient-free global methods help with multimodal problems of modest dimension.</p>","50.2":"<p>Hypersonic design couples aerodynamics, propulsion, structures, thermal protection, controls and trajectory.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Architecture</th> <th>Idea</th> <th>Trade-off</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>MDF (multidisciplinary feasible)</td> <td>Converge the full multidisciplinary analysis at every optimizer iteration</td> <td>Simple and always consistent; expensive</td> </tr> <tr> <td>IDF (individual discipline feasible)</td> <td>Coupling variables become design variables with consistency constraints</td> <td>Disciplines run in parallel; consistent only at convergence</td> </tr> <tr> <td>AAO / SAND</td> <td>Optimizer drives all residuals to zero simultaneously</td> <td>Most efficient for tightly coupled problems; intrusive</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Distributed (CO, BLISS, ATC)</td> <td>Disciplines optimize locally under system-level coordination</td> <td>Mirrors organizations; can converge slowly</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Frameworks such as NASA’s open-source OpenMDAO implement these with analytic derivatives.</p>","50.3":"<p>Finite differences cost <span class=\"mi\">n + 1</span> analyses and face the step-size dilemma (Chapter 14). The <strong>complex step</strong> <span class=\"mi\">f'(x) \\approx Im\\left\\lbrack f(x + ih) \\right\\rbrack/h</span> involves no subtraction, so <span class=\"mi\">h = 10^{- 20}</span> gives derivatives exact to machine precision (the code must accept complex numbers). <em>Algorithmic differentiation</em> applies the chain rule to code: forward mode costs scale with the number of inputs, reverse mode with the number of outputs.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Adjoint method.</strong> For a model with residual equations <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{R}\\left( \\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{x} \\right) = 0</span> and objective <span class=\"mi\">J\\left( \\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{x} \\right)</span>: solve <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{R}}{\\partial\\mathbf{u}} \\right)^{T}\\mathbf{\\psi} = \\left( \\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial\\mathbf{u}} \\right)^{T}</span> once, then <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{dJ}{d\\mathbf{x}} = \\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial\\mathbf{x}} - \\mathbf{\\psi}^{T}\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{R}}{\\partial\\mathbf{x}}</span>. The cost is one extra solve per <em>objective</em>, independent of the number of design variables. This makes aerodynamic shape optimization with thousands of variables practical; coupled adjoints extend it to multidisciplinary systems.</p></div>","50.4":"<p>Sample the design space with full factorial designs, Latin hypercubes or Sobol sequences. Fit polynomial response surfaces, radial basis functions, <em>Gaussian-process (kriging)</em> models (which report their own uncertainty, enabling efficient global optimization by expected improvement), neural networks or reduced-order models (POD, Chapter 10). <em>Multi-fidelity</em> methods (for example co-kriging) combine many cheap analyses (engineering methods, Euler) with a few expensive ones (RANS, tests).</p>","50.5":"<p>Uncertainty enters through inputs, model form and numerics. It may be aleatory or epistemic (Chapter 13); epistemic parts may be treated as intervals or probability boxes. <strong>Forward propagation:</strong> Monte Carlo (error <span class=\"mi\">\\propto 1/\\sqrt{N}</span>, independent of dimension); Latin-hypercube and quasi-Monte Carlo sampling (faster convergence); <strong>polynomial chaos expansions</strong>, which represent the output in orthogonal polynomials of the inputs (Hermite for Gaussian, Legendre for uniform inputs); stochastic collocation on sparse grids. <strong>Global sensitivity:</strong> the first-order Sobol index <span class=\"mi\">S_{i} = Var\\left( E\\left\\lbrack Y|X_{i} \\right\\rbrack \\right)/Var(Y)</span> is the share of output variance due to <span class=\"mi\">X_{i}</span> alone; the total index <span class=\"mi\">S_{T_{i}}</span> includes all its interactions. Morris screening ranks many inputs cheaply.</p>","50.6":"<p>Failure occurs when a limit-state function <span class=\"mi\">g\\left( \\mathbf{x} \\right) \\leq 0</span>. In the <strong>first-order reliability method</strong> (FORM), transform the inputs to independent standard normals, find the most probable failure point, and take its distance from the origin as the reliability index <span class=\"mi\">\\beta</span>, so that <span class=\"mi\">P_{f} \\approx \\Phi( - \\beta)</span>. SORM adds curvature corrections; importance sampling handles rare events. For <span class=\"mi\">g = R - S</span> with independent normal strength <span class=\"mi\">R</span> and load <span class=\"mi\">S</span>: <span class=\"mi\">\\beta = \\left( \\mu_{R} - \\mu_{S} \\right)/\\sqrt{\\sigma_{R}^{2} + \\sigma_{S}^{2}}</span>.</p>","50.7":"<p><em>Robust design</em> optimizes the mean and penalizes the variance of performance. <em>Reliability-based design optimization</em> constrains failure probabilities directly. Both make explicit what traditional factors of safety (Chapter 33) leave implicit.</p>","50.8":"<p>Bayesian calibration (Kennedy–O’Hagan) infers model parameters and a <em>model-discrepancy</em> term from data. Validation metrics, such as the area between predicted and measured cumulative distributions, quantify agreement. Predictions then carry model-form uncertainty into decisions.</p>","50.9":"<p>A concept “closes” when the mass it needs (operating empty weight plus payload plus fuel, <span class=\"mi\">W_{TOGW} = W_{OEW} + W_{pay} + W_{fuel}</span>) and the volume it needs (fuel and systems) are both satisfied by the same geometry. Key parameters are Küchemann’s volume parameter <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = V_{tot}/S_{pln}^{3/2}</span>, a structural index (structural mass per unit wetted area), engine Isp and L/D. Czysz’s <em>Hypersonic Convergence</em> method maps the solution space where volume and mass closures intersect, exposing sensitivity to technology assumptions. Mature design couples aerodynamics, propulsion, thermal, structures and trajectory in MDO with uncertainty-driven margins.</p>","50.10":"<p>Model-based systems engineering (SysML v2) replaces document-centric specifications with connected models. The <em>digital thread</em> carries model-based definitions through manufacturing, test and operations. A <em>digital twin</em> is a physics-based model of a specific vehicle, continually updated with its own data, used for health monitoring and life prediction. The credibility of these models rests on the verification, validation and UQ practices above.</p>"}}}});